25:The predicate has one soundness worry worth retiring now: it compares a filesystem-canonicalized path against git's own spelling of the toplevel, and those are produced by two different tools. A live probe against this repository shows them agreeing byte-for-byte, and the failure mode if they ever disagree is the right one — inequality can only deny rooting, so a spelling divergence refuses an honest census loudly instead of accepting an unrooted one silently. Two hardenings close the gap: look_grep itself compares the canonicalized search root against the marker's root at capture time and stores the search entry's key in the marker's spelling when both name the same directory, leaving check a pure byte comparison; and the agreement is pinned by a test rather than assumed, since one probe on one machine establishes the common case, not an invariant.
84:| C2 | TET-28's "workspace-rooted" requirement is NOT decidable from today's capture in the case that matters most. When look_grep finds matches, it records one extent entry per matched file, keyed on that file's resolved path, with label "<file> (grep: <pattern>)" — and the search root appears nowhere in what is captured; the code's own comment says the entries are keyed "not by the search root or the grep command line". A repo-wide grep and a single-file grep that matched the same file therefore capture byte-identical extents. Every genuine census of a symbol an implementer is told to modify falls in this positive-match case, because the symbol's definition site matches. | OWED |
86:| C4 | The census therefore needs a small capture extension before any refusal can exist: look_grep should record, for every search, one root-level search entry — key = the resolved search root, a label carrying the pattern and the match-file count, marker resolved from the search root — alongside the existing per-file match entries, making the match and no-match cases uniform. "Workspace-rooted" then has one decidable definition covering both cases: the search entry's key equals its own world_root, a byte comparison of two strings the same look_grep call captured from the same canonicalized directory. No author-typed input participates on either side. | OWED |
92:| C10 | The rooting predicate — census entry's key byte-equals its own world_root — is satisfiable and fails closed. On this machine and repo the two spellings (fs-canonicalized search root; git rev-parse --show-toplevel) agree byte-for-byte, so an honestly rooted grep satisfies the predicate; and if the two tools ever disagree on spelling, the mismatch refuses an honest census loudly rather than accepting an unrooted one silently, because inequality can only ever deny rooting, never grant it. Two hardenings belong in the implementation: look_grep should itself compare the canonicalized search root against the marker's root at capture time and store the search entry's key in the marker's spelling when they name the same directory, so check stays a pure byte comparison; and the agreement should be pinned by a test, since one probe on one machine shows the common case, not an invariant. | OWED |
95:| C13 | The feature's implementation surface is small: one additional recorded entry in look_grep, two serde-defaulted structured fields on the extent entry (following world_root's own compatibility precedent), one new append-only record type whose authoring verb refuses uncensused declarations from one dispatch shared by CLI and MCP, one rendered section beside the existing ledger and Facts tables, and one machine check with its human-owed and non-coverage lines. Each component extends a mechanism this memo's facts describe, and none of them is a code-to-graph link, a commit reference, or dependency propagation — the mechanisms the TET-27 ticket describes as its subject — so shipping this first imposes no design debt on that exploration. | OWED |
97:| C15 | A census enumerates call sites, not the modes those sites select, and this is a distinct printed limit rather than the insincere-examination ceiling restated. Distinct because the two limits bound different things: the ceiling bounds interpretation — capture makes the extent honest, not the reading of it — while the mode limit bounds the artifact itself. What look_grep captures per matched file is the matching lines only; an argument that selects materially different behaviour (a None versus a Some, a flag, a mode enum) is sometimes visible on a match line, never enumerated, and absent entirely when the value arrives through a variable, a default, or another line of a multi-line call. So even a sincerely read census does not, in general, contain which mechanism each caller engages — the reader must leave the census and read the callee and each call's context, which is why collapsing this into "whether anyone read the census" would misstate the remedy. It does not change the design: surfacing argument values at census time would require parsing call expressions, which is per-language knowledge — exactly the coupling the project's language-agnostic-by-construction doctrine exists to exclude — and it would not change the trigger either, since a value-surfacing census would still need the same declared-target trigger. It costs one more line in the census's non-coverage: the census gets an implementer to the door of every textual call site; it does not say what happens inside any of them. | OWED |
106:| F2 | How look/run capture the working tree and, crucially, what a grep records. worldstate.rs: every observation carries a Marker { root, state } where root is `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` resolved per-observation from the path the observation touched (canonicalized; NO_GIT outside a repo), and state hashes HEAD plus `git diff HEAD --binary`. observe.rs look_grep: (a) it passes -H unconditionally so single-file greps key on a real filename; (b) a ZERO-match grep records one NoMatch entry whose key is resolve_key(search root) and whose label is "no-match: <pattern> in <root>", with the marker resolved from the search root; (c) a grep WITH matches records one GrepMatch entry per matched file, keyed on the matched file's resolved path with label "<file> (grep: <pattern>)", and the SEARCH ROOT IS NOT RECORDED ANYWHERE in those entries — the comment says explicitly "keyed by that file's resolved path (fix 2), not by the search root or the grep command line". So for a positive-match grep, nothing captured distinguishes a repo-wide search from a single-file search that happened to match the same files. look_path records the opened file's key/label and per-file marker; run records key=cmdline, label "proc: <cmdline> (exit N)", marker from the process cwd. | /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/worldstate.rs; /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/observe.rs | C2, C3, C4, C5, C10, C11, C12, C13, C15 |
