the ledger, and go out of proof on revision until regraded (checks.rs's out_of_proof machine failure).\",\"pin\":\"sha256:0a7fd36de509936ec5d5cb4e1bc0286b9c19842c50dd8385a9c99d06a01763dc\",\"timestamp\":1786175478}}\n46:{\"_type\":\"https://in-toto.io/Statement/v1\",\"subject\":[{\"name\":\"C14\",\"digest\":{\"sha256\":\"799970d9eef0666bafdb66241e30dac5157b6ee0e1826c905f6c09581fbbf5de\"}}],\"predicateType\":\"https://github.com/lonic-software/tetel/captured-fact/v1\",\"predicate\":{\"verdict\":\"supports\",\"pass\":\"c7ccb4bc34bdb0bf\",\"reported_kind\":\"run\",\"source\":\"proc:workspace-c7ccb4bc34bdb0bf\",\"extent\":[\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/compose.rs\",\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/snapshot.rs\",\"no-match: --out in /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/main.rs\",\"no-match: NON_COVERAGE in /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/checks.rs\",\"no-match: never declared themselves in /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/checks.rs\",\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/checks.rs (grep: uncensused)\",\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/report.rs (grep: NON_COVERAGE)\",\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/report.rs (grep: declared themselves)\",\"no-match: out_path in /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src\",\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/report.rs lines 1-60\",\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/main.rs (grep: render)\",\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/main.rs lines 232-268\",\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/main.rs lines 700-745\",\"no-match: runs nothing in /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/checks.rs\",\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/checks.rs (grep: unverifiable)\",\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/checks.rs lines 1220-1310\",\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/checks.rs lines 130-215\"],\"world\":[{\"root\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel\",\"state\":\"git:5076b5fe177f2ab87259d8d1e36881c055e0be35001992b01789e8ac000461a8\"}],\"note\":\"Each named extension point exists in the shape claimed: SNAPSHOT_FILES is a deliberately-enumerated array whose targets.jsonl entry documents, verbatim, that without shipping the log the census refusal \\\"would hold only inside the workspace that authored the memo\\\"; render_targets is the section to render beside; render --out is the completeness-refusal site; census_findings is the shipped both-directions re-verification pattern running no commands; NON_COVERAGE is the printed list to extend; and report.rs's contract has \\\"no standalone document-level verdict anywhere\\\", matching the no-aggregate pledge.\",\"pin\":\"sha256:0a7fd36de509936ec5d5cb4e1bc0286b9c19842c50dd8385a9c99d06a01763dc\",\"timestamp\":1786175496}}\n12:{\"event\":\"Create\",\"id\":\"C12\",\"prop\":\"What the mechanism cannot catch is boundable and belongs in check's printed NON_COVERAGE list, which exists for exactly this (\\\"a refusal that fired is easily read as coverage it never claimed\\\"). Six entries: (a) a transplant the author never declared — no refusal can reach it; the empty section is its only trace; (b) premises the donor never wrote down — the relation selects from captured text, so a premise that lives in the donor's structure rather than its comments has nothing to select; (c) premises outside the captured extent — a donor fact that folded too narrow a look cannot yield a premise from the lines it never captured; (d) shallow selection — the tool verifies that selected bytes are the donor's, never that they are all the load-bearing ones; selecting one convenient clause and stopping is invisible to any format-level check, because deciding what counts as a premise is design judgment (the ticket says so) and deciding a selection is too small is a truth-check, the insincere-examination ceiling this project registered as permanently out of reach; (e) whether the discharge claim actually addresses its premise, and whether the cited target really is the mechanism's destination — both content; the claim is graded by a grounding pass as written, and the linkage is graded by nobody; (f) a donor comment that is itself wrong — verbatim quotation establishes provenance, never truth. None of these is reachable by a format-level refusal, so printing them is the honest disposition, not a fallback.\",\"from\":[\"F7\",\"F3\"],\"timestamp\":1786173455}\n14:{\"event\":\"Create\",\"id\":\"C14\",\"prop\":\"The implementation surface is small and every piece extends a mechanism already in the tree: one serde-defaulted per-entry output-length field on ExtentEntry (the same compatibility precedent that added kind and pattern, with the same absent-means-ineligible reading); one new record kind with its counter and id prefix; one transplants.jsonl appended by one CLI/MCP-shared dispatch, shipped by adding one line to snapshot.rs's deliberately-enumerated SNAPSHOT_FILES (whose targets.jsonl entry already documents why a record log must ship: without it the refusal \\\"would hold only inside the workspace that authored the memo\\\"); one rendered section beside render_targets; one completeness refusal in the render --out path; one check re-verifying from files the snapshot already carries (transplant machine findings beside uncensused_targets, unverifiable rows human-owed without a snapshot); and the new NON_COVERAGE lines. Nothing parses any programming language, nothing is re-run at check time, and no aggregate, score, or document-level verdict is introduced anywhere.\",\"from\":[\"F2\",\"F5\",\"F7\",\"F8\"],\"timestamp\":1786173478}\n18:{\"event\":\"Revise\",\"id\":\"C12\",\"prop\":\"What the mechanism cannot catch is boundable and belongs in check's printed NON_COVERAGE list, which exists for exactly this (\\\"a refusal that fired is easily read as coverage it never claimed\\\"). Six entries: (a) a transplant the author never declared — no refusal can reach it; the empty section is its only trace; (b) premises the donor never wrote down — the relation selects from captured text, so a premise that lives in the donor's structure rather than its comments has nothing to select; (c) premises outside the captured extent — a donor fact that folded too narrow a look cannot yield a premise from the lines it never captured; (d) shallow selection — the tool verifies that selected bytes are the donor's, never that they are all the load-bearing ones; selecting one convenient clause and stopping is invisible to any format-level check: \\\"what counts as a premise is design judgment\\\" is a boundary the ticket itself draws and this design records as the ticket's stipulation, and deciding a selection is too small is a truth-check, the insincere-examination ceiling this project registered as permanently out of reach; (e) whether the discharge claim actually addresses its premise, and whether the cited target really is the mechanism's destination — both content; the claim is graded by a grounding pass as written, and the linkage is graded by nobody; (f) a donor comment that is itself wrong — verbatim quotation establishes provenance, never truth. None of these is reachable by a format-level refusal, so printing them is the honest disposition, not a fallback.\",\"from\":null,\"why\":\"Grounding pass k1 qualified this claim on the clause \\\"(the ticket says so)\\\", which rests on a document outside any grounding pass's bound. The attribution is now explicit: the boundary is recorded as the ticket's stipulation, not established from source, matching how C13 already carries ticket material.\",\"timestamp\":1786174615}\n8:{\"event\":\"Create\",\"id\":\"F7\",\"note\":\"The assembly-and-verification surfaces TET-29 extends, first-hand. compose.rs: render appends render_targets between the prose and the evidence ledger; the section renders \\\"None declared...\\\" once any claim exists (\\\"the hole is the finding\\\" — \\\"an absent section would make the same omission invisible, which is the failure mode, not the fix\\\"); withdrawn targets are dropped, live ones render as a two-column table |`symbol`|F#|; only labels are rendered, never absolute keys (\\\"a committed document should not carry /Users/<someone>/...\\\"), and captured output is deliberately NOT rendered into the document (\\\"unbounded ... it is in the snapshot for anyone who needs it, and a document that inlined it would bury the prose\\\"). checks.rs: Findings has uncensused_targets as a MACHINE failure (\\\"re-verified against the shipped snapshot ... what this catches is the document that never passed through that verb: hand-authored, edited after rendering, or carrying a target row its own snapshot does not have. Both directions are checked\\\"), and unverifiable_targets as human-owed when no snapshot shipped; machine_check_failed() ORs grammar/subset/abutting/unsettled/cascade/ledger/verdict-disagreement/out-of-proof/uncensused-targets/provenance; out_of_proof (every record grades a wording the claim no longer carries) is a machine failure whose remedy is reproving. report.rs: exit codes 0/1/2; NON_COVERAGE is a const list printed item by item (\\\"tetel does not catch: ...\\\"), and TET-28 added five census entries to it with a comment explaining why (\\\"a refusal that fired is easily read as coverage it never claimed\\\"); first entry is \\\"dependents that never declared themselves\\\". snapshot.rs: the snapshot copies facts.jsonl, claims.jsonl, prose.jsonl and targets.jsonl beside the memo, so a transplant log shipped the same way would be re-verifiable by check from files the snapshot already carries.\",\"extent\":[{\"key\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/compose.rs\",\"label\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/compose.rs lines 1-130\",\"world_root\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel\",\"world_state\":\"git:5076b5fe177f2ab87259d8d1e36881c055e0be35001992b01789e8ac000461a8\"},{\"key\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/checks.rs\",\"label\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/checks.rs lines 120-215\",\"world_root\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel\",\"world_state\":\"git:5076b5fe177f2ab87259d8d1e36881c055e0be35001992b01789e8ac000461a8\"},{\"key\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/report.rs\",\"label\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/report.rs lines 13-40\",\"world_root\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel\",\"world_state\":\"git:5076b5fe177f2ab87259d8d1e36881c055e0be35001992b01789e8ac000461a8\"}],\"output\":\"//! `tetel render` — assemble the workspace's current prose state into\\n//! markdown on stdout, followed by an evidence ledger built from the\\n//! workspace's claims. Mirrors `trender`, plus fix 1 from this port's own\\n//! design memo: the prototype's `trender` had nothing downstream that\\n//! read its output back, so a rendered document and `tetel check` never\\n//! connected — every check reported \\\"no evidence ledger found\\\" against\\n//! a document this same tool had just produced. Appending a ledger\\n//! table in the shape `ledger::import` already reads closes that loop\\n//! without inventing a third evidence shape.\\n//!\\n//! Headings render at their own declared depth (`#` * level), not a\\n//! single fixed depth — see `prose.rs`'s doc comment on why that's a\\n//! fix, not a stylistic choice. A revised block renders only its\\n//! current text; superseded text lives in `prose.jsonl`'s history but\\n//! never here.\\n//!\\n//! The ledger is strictly an addition after the prose: nothing here can\\n//! change a single byte the prose loop already wrote, so the document a\\n//! human reads stays exactly what was authored — only `tetel check`\\n//! sees the appended table as evidence to grade.\\n\\nuse std::io;\\nuse std::path::Path;\\n\\nuse crate::claims;\\nuse crate::prose;\\n\\npub fn render(workspace_dir: &Path) -> io::Result<String> {\\n    let blocks = prose::load_all(workspace_dir)?;\\n    let mut out = String::new();\\n    for (i, b) in blocks.iter().enumerate() {\\n        if i > 0 {\\n            out.push('\\\\n');\\n        }\\n        if b.heading {\\n            let level = b.level.unwrap_or(2).clamp(1, 6);\\n            out.push_str(&\\\"#\\\".repeat(level as usize));\\n            out.push(' ');\\n            out.push_str(&b.text);\\n            out.push('\\\\n');\\n        } else {\\n            out.push_str(&b.text);\\n            out.push('\\\\n');\\n            if !b.cite.is_empty() {\\n                out.push('\\\\n');\\n                out.push_str(&format!(\\\"*cites: {}*\\\\n\\\", b.cite.join(\\\", \\\")));\\n            }\\n        }\\n    }\\n    let claim_list = claims::load_all(workspace_dir)?;\\n    out.push_str(&render_targets(&crate::targets::load_all(workspace_dir)?, &claim_list));\\n    out.push_str(&render_ledger(&claim_list));\\n    out.push_str(&render_facts(\\n        &crate::facts::load_all(workspace_dir)?,\\n        &claim_list,\\n    ));\\n    Ok(out)\\n}\\n\\n/// Appends the modification-target section — the symbols this design\\n/// tells an implementer to modify, each with the fact that censuses it.\\n///\\n/// # Why it renders when it is empty\\n///\\n/// Because the hole is the finding. A declared target is refused without\\n/// a census at declaration time, so a *declared* target is always\\n/// censused; what no refusal can reach is a target the author never\\n/// declared, since deciding that a memo recommended something it never\\n/// declared is the heuristic read of prose the whole design rejects.\\n///\\n/// Visibility is the answer instead. Once a document has any claim at\\n/// all, this section renders — as \\\"none declared\\\" when it is empty — so\\n/// a memo full of tell-the-implementer language with an empty section\\n/// shows its own gap to every reader. An absent section would make the\\n/// same omission invisible, which is the failure mode, not the fix.\\n///\\n/// Withdrawn targets are dropped rather than struck through: a target is\\n/// withdrawn precisely when it stopped being a target, and the log keeps\\n/// the history for anyone auditing the workspace.\\nfn render_targets(targets: &[crate::targets::Target], claims: &[claims::Claim]) -> String {\\n    let live: Vec<&crate::targets::Target> = targets.iter().filter(|t| !t.withdrawn).collect();\\n    // Nothing to be silent about yet: a document with no claims is not a\\n    // document making recommendations.\\n    if live.is_empty() && claims.is_empty() {\\n        return String::new();\\n    }\\n    let mut out = String::from(\\\"\\\\n## Modification targets\\\\n\\\\n\\\");\\n    if live.is_empty() {\\n        out.push_str(\\n            \\\"None declared. A target is a symbol this design tells an implementer to modify; \\\\\\n             declaring one requires a search of the whole worktree for it. Nothing forces a \\\\\\n             recommendation to be declared, so an empty section is not evidence that none exist.\\\\n\\\",\\n        );\\n        return out;\\n    }\\n    out.push_str(\\\"| target | censused by |\\\\n|---|---|\\\\n\\\");\\n    for t in live {\\n        out.push_str(&format!(\\\"| `{}` | {} |\\\\n\\\", t.symbol, t.from));\\n    }\\n    out\\n}\\n\\n/// Appends a facts table after the evidence ledger.\\n///\\n/// # Why the document has to carry this\\n///\\n/// Before it did, `prose --cites` accepted fact ids as readily as claim\\n/// ids and `render` wrote them into the document, but no fact appeared\\n/// anywhere in the output — so `tetel check` reported `cited but\\n/// undefined: [F5]` on the first real memo, correctly, and would have on\\n/// every memo that ever cited a fact from prose.\\n///\\n/// The deeper reason is what a reader loses without it. A fact's captured\\n/// extent is the one field in this system no author can type, and reading\\n/// notes against extents is where review value has actually shown up —\\n/// the FORK-94 review's sharpest finding was a note concluding about a\\n/// file its extent never covered. Without this table that comparison is\\n/// possible only by opening the workspace, which a reviewer of a\\n/// committed document does not have.\\n///\\n/// # What is deliberately not here\\n///\\n/// The `key` is an absolute machine-local path; the `label` is\\n/// repo-relative. Only the label is rendered — a committed document\\n/// should not carry `/Users/<someone>/...`, and the label is what a\\n/// reader can act on anyway.\\n///\\n/// Captured output is not rendered either. It is unbounded (a `run` can\\n/// capture megabytes), it is in the snapshot for anyone who needs it, and\\n/// a document that inlined it would bury the prose it exists to support.\\n    /// two facts asserting opposite things about the same code disagree\\n    /// about the world or about the same world, which before this the\\n    /// ledger could not answer at all. Same snapshot dependency as\\n    /// `mint_windows`.\\n    pub tree_states: Vec<crate::worldstate::RootStates>,\\n    /// Facts whose markers predate roots being recorded, so their tree\\n    /// state describes the process's working directory rather than what\\n    /// they read. Reported rather than passed over: an ungradable record\\n    /// is not a matching one.\\n    pub tree_ungradable: Vec<String>,\\n    /// Declared modification targets whose cited fact does not census\\n    /// them, re-verified against the shipped snapshot — a **machine\\n    /// failure**.\\n    ///\\n    /// `tetel target` refuses these at declaration, so a workspace-\\n    /// authored memo cannot produce one. What this catches is the\\n    /// document that never passed through that verb: hand-authored,\\n    /// edited after rendering, or carrying a target row its own snapshot\\n    /// does not have. Both directions are checked, because tampering can\\n    /// go either way — a row invented in the document, or a target whose\\n    /// citation stopped censusing it.\\n    ///\\n    /// It is an objective contradiction between a document and the record\\n    /// it claims to rest on, which is what the machine partition is for.\\n    pub uncensused_targets: Vec<String>,\\n    /// Target rows in a document that shipped no snapshot, so nothing\\n    /// can verify them — **human-owed**, never a failure.\\n    ///\\n    /// Same standing choice as every other snapshot-dependent finding: a\\n    /// missing snapshot grades the tooling's history rather than the\\n    /// document, and every memo authored before `render --out` existed\\n    /// lacks one.\\n    pub unverifiable_targets: Vec<String>,\\n    /// True when the memo's ledger has no scope columns for any claim to\\n    /// declare into — a tetel-authored ledger. Reported once at document\\n    /// level rather than once per claim; see\\n    /// [`claims_without_declared_scope`] for why that changed.\\n    pub ledger_has_no_scope_columns: bool,\\n    /// One line per grounded claim describing how its evidence stands:\\n    /// witnessed (extent captured by this tool, in a named workspace) or\\n    /// ingested (extent typed by a caller). Human-owed, printed item by\\n    /// item, never a failure — see [`grounding_provenance`].\\n    pub grounding_provenance: Vec<String>,\\n    /// `(claim id, pass, note)` per record whose verdict is\\n    /// [`Verdict::Qualifies`]. Human-owed, never a failure.\\n    ///\\n    /// Until this existed, a qualifying verdict was invisible. On the\\n    /// first real independent grounding pass, the two claims the grounder\\n    /// declined to confirm — because the numbers they rest on could not\\n    /// be verified from what it was given — printed as plainly \\\"grounded\\\",\\n    /// while the one claim where it qualified a single premise of a\\n    /// multi-premise argument was the only thing reddening the report.\\n    /// The check fired on the wrong claim and was silent on the right\\n    /// ones.\\n    pub qualified_claims: Vec<(String, String, String)>,\\n    /// Claims **out of proof**: every record on file grades a wording this\\n    /// claim no longer carries, and nothing has graded the current one.\\n    /// The proof-house sense exactly — the stamp no longer certifies this\\n    /// barrel. A machine failure; see [`analyze_ledger`].\\n    ///\\n    /// The remedy is to **reprove**: ground the claim again against what it\\n    /// now says. Nothing else clears it, and nothing clears it silently —\\n    /// the ledger is append-only, so the superseding record is added rather\\n    /// than the stale one edited.\\n    pub out_of_proof: Vec<String>,\\n    /// Records that graded an earlier wording of a claim which *has* since\\n    /// been reproved. Human-owed history, never a failure — the claim is\\n    /// back in proof, and these are the marks from before it was. See\\n    /// [`analyze_ledger`] on why the distinction matters.\\n    pub superseded_evidence: Vec<String>,\\n}\\n\\nimpl Findings {\\n    pub fn machine_check_failed(&self) -> bool {\\n        !self.grammar_errors.is_empty()\\n            || !self.subset_failures.is_empty()\\n            || !self.abutting_failures.is_empty()\\n            || !self.unsettled_failures.is_empty()\\n            || !self.cascade_failures.is_empty()\\n            || !self.ledger_errors.is_empty()\\n            || !self.verdict_disagreements.is_empty()\\n            || !self.out_of_proof.is_empty()\\n            || !self.uncensused_targets.is_empty()\\n            || self.provenance_failed()\\n    }\\n\\n    /// Drift and an unreadable snapshot are machine failures: both are\\n    /// objective contradictions between a document and the record it\\n    /// claims to rest on, decidable without a human.\\n    ///\\n    /// A *missing* snapshot is deliberately not a failure. Every memo\\n    /// authored before `render --out` existed lacks one, and turning that\\n    /// into a hard failure would grade the tooling's own history rather\\n    /// than the document. It is reported as human-owed instead.\\n    pub fn provenance_failed(&self) -> bool {\\n        matches!(\\npub const EXIT_CHECK_FAILED: i32 = 1;\\npub const EXIT_NO_ROWS: i32 = 2;\\n\\nconst NON_COVERAGE: &[&str] = &[\\n    \\\"dependents that never declared themselves\\\",\\n    \\\"deleted premises\\\",\\n    \\\"one word used in two senses\\\",\\n    \\\"a command that runs green without establishing its proposition\\\",\\n    \\\"unfalsifiable-shaped claim phrasing\\\",\\n    // --- what a modification-target census cannot see ------------------\\n    // The census refusal is textual and rooted, and each of these is a\\n    // way a use of a symbol escapes a textual search or a way a found\\n    // site still tells you nothing. Printed rather than left implicit,\\n    // because a refusal that fired is easily read as coverage it never\\n    // claimed.\\n    \\\"uses of a censused symbol reached by dynamic dispatch, re-export, aliased import, \\\\\\nor a name assembled by a macro or string concatenation\\\",\\n    \\\"whatever the platform's grep skipped — symlinked directories and binary files bound \\\\\\nwhat a whole-worktree search physically visited\\\",\\n    \\\"whether the censused worktree is the tree this design is actually about; rooting says \\\\\\nwhere a search started, never that it started in the right place\\\",\\n    \\\"which mode a call site selects: a census enumerates sites, never the argument values \\\\\\nthose sites pass, so a parameter choosing between materially different mechanisms is \\\\\\ninvisible even to a careful reader of the census\\\",\\n    \\\"a modification target the author never declared — no refusal can reach it, and the \\\\\\nempty section is its only trace\\\",\\n];\\n\",\"pin\":\"sha256:ef8efea246d0124d91d2f89c04588cafbdd754f08241b5be564412d2cce7a064\",\"timestamp\":1786172878}\n10:{\"event\":\"Revise\",\"id\":\"F7\",\"note\":\"The assembly-and-verification surfaces TET-29 extends, first-hand. compose.rs: render appends render_targets between the prose and the evidence ledger; the section renders \\\"None declared...\\\" once any claim exists (\\\"the hole is the finding\\\" — \\\"an absent section would make the same omission invisible, which is the failure mode, not the fix\\\"); withdrawn targets are dropped, live ones render as a two-column table |`symbol`|F#|; only labels are rendered, never absolute keys (\\\"a committed document should not carry /Users/<someone>/...\\\"), and captured output is deliberately NOT rendered into the document (\\\"unbounded ... it is in the snapshot for anyone who needs it, and a document that inlined it would bury the prose\\\"). checks.rs: Findings has uncensused_targets as a MACHINE failure (\\\"re-verified against the shipped snapshot ... what this catches is the document that never passed through that verb: hand-authored, edited after rendering, or carrying a target row its own snapshot does not have. Both directions are checked\\\"), and unverifiable_targets as human-owed when no snapshot shipped; machine_check_failed() ORs grammar/subset/abutting/unsettled/cascade/ledger/verdict-disagreement/out-of-proof/uncensused-targets/provenance; out_of_proof (every record grades a wording the claim no longer carries) is a machine failure whose remedy is reproving. report.rs: exit codes 0/1/2; NON_COVERAGE is a const list printed item by item (\\\"tetel does not catch: ...\\\"), and TET-28 added five census entries to it with a comment explaining why (\\\"a refusal that fired is easily read as coverage it never claimed\\\"); first entry is \\\"dependents that never declared themselves\\\".\",\"why\":\"The note's last sentence concluded about snapshot.rs, which this fact's extent (compose.rs, checks.rs, report.rs spans) does not cover — flagged by the mint's attention line. snapshot.rs is now read first-hand as F8; this note keeps only what its own extent shows.\",\"timestamp\":1786172905}\n16:const NON_COVERAGE: &[&str] = &[\n319:    for item in NON_COVERAGE {\n19:Completeness refuses at `render --out`, not at the verb. An unanswered\n23:toward never declaring it. A preview render still assembles and marks the\n40:shared dispatch. This render path has already shipped one CLI/MCP\n44:Both design memos are re-rendered, and what that exposed is worth\n46:They were rendered over an MCP server holding a pre-TET-28 binary, so\n48:generally, any change to what `render` emits retroactively drifts every\n50:re-render of its snapshot. Prose here is byte-identical and the ledger\n29:5b766137c48a10c6a068e272251438dc47c9f046 e511c0eca946c3587e64bbe2722aab46a47fd207 kolonicsmate <mate.kolonics@rabit.hu> 1785971379 +0200\tcommit: tetel prose and tetel render: compose the document one block at a time\n33:ca114f7e83441c492a0772fe8c90e95b9cc2e463 33efab332e83db423842f162c2615e52d7696d22 kolonicsmate <mate.kolonics@rabit.hu> 1785996063 +0200\tcommit: render: append a checkable evidence ledger, so check stops saying no evidence ledger found\n44:68bb6cb0e812b0d143ff3e55ff85871613e4b408 1428683fa0bbdd3e1f87db25c66db556f67a5096 kolonicsmate <mate.kolonics@rabit.hu> 1786005031 +0200\tcommit: check: read render's own citation syntax, and unify the flag that writes it\n45:1428683fa0bbdd3e1f87db25c66db556f67a5096 cea4741e8854efa5ee38dfae14b3040e48682dc8 kolonicsmate <mate.kolonics@rabit.hu> 1786009123 +0200\tcommit: render --out: ship the workspace snapshot with the document, and check drift\n49:52d629f82f951fa2b496db78a63bb0599915c119 2ab167a3646aecfe468cdb06d9538ac143c5cef7 kolonicsmate <mate.kolonics@rabit.hu> 1786012859 +0200\tcommit: render a facts table, and stop reading bracketed Rust as citations\n86:5f53b8ec8192277a860ceef97eb199bc0182d48d faded8f90139521b6b6eba01ec3077698ed2ce01 kolonicsmate <mate.kolonics@rabit.hu> 1786123567 +0200\tcommit: say what tetel is about, and stop leading with the render target\n93:7594f00b320493d3bc6041a797bc26c6f7f210bd 2a7bfe3e75187e0fa0bb25827dc6c7abbb803ed3 kolonicsmate <mate.kolonics@rabit.hu> 1786135128 +0200\tcommit: design: stop rendering every TET-28 heading twice\n6:5b766137c48a10c6a068e272251438dc47c9f046 e511c0eca946c3587e64bbe2722aab46a47fd207 kolonicsmate <mate.kolonics@rabit.hu> 1785971379 +0200\tcommit: tetel prose and tetel render: compose the document one block at a time\n10:ca114f7e83441c492a0772fe8c90e95b9cc2e463 33efab332e83db423842f162c2615e52d7696d22 kolonicsmate <mate.kolonics@rabit.hu> 1785996063 +0200\tcommit: render: append a checkable evidence ledger, so check stops saying no evidence ledger found\n5:68bb6cb0e812b0d143ff3e55ff85871613e4b408 1428683fa0bbdd3e1f87db25c66db556f67a5096 kolonicsmate <mate.kolonics@rabit.hu> 1786005031 +0200\tcommit: check: read render's own citation syntax, and unify the flag that writes it\n6:1428683fa0bbdd3e1f87db25c66db556f67a5096 cea4741e8854efa5ee38dfae14b3040e48682dc8 kolonicsmate <mate.kolonics@rabit.hu> 1786009123 +0200\tcommit: render --out: ship the workspace snapshot with the document, and check drift\n10:52d629f82f951fa2b496db78a63bb0599915c119 2ab167a3646aecfe468cdb06d9538ac143c5cef7 kolonicsmate <mate.kolonics@rabit.hu> 1786012859 +0200\tcommit: render a facts table, and stop reading bracketed Rust as citations\n46:5f53b8ec8192277a860ceef97eb199bc0182d48d faded8f90139521b6b6eba01ec3077698ed2ce01 kolonicsmate <mate.kolonics@rabit.hu> 1786123567 +0200\tcommit: say what tetel is about, and stop leading with the render target\n53:7594f00b320493d3bc6041a797bc26c6f7f210bd 2a7bfe3e75187e0fa0bb25827dc6c7abbb803ed3 kolonicsmate <mate.kolonics@rabit.hu> 1786135128 +0200\tcommit: design: stop rendering every TET-28 heading twice\n70:tetel render --out design.md               # the document, plus its snapshot\n74:Authoring is `look` / `run` → `fact` → `claim` → `prose` → `render`, with `target` alongside for\n91:| `design.md` | the rendered document — prose plus evidence rows, plain markdown so agents read it, tooling greps it, and review happens in a diff |\n96:Markdown is what `render` emits, not what tetel is about. **The render target is the most replaceable\n118:| **discharge** | the claim asserting a premise holds at the destination. `render --out` refuses a document with a premise nothing answers |\n3:All code observations in this memo were taken against the tetel repository at commit 57bd0e2 (\"ci: make the guards authoritative for the repository, not just for one editor\"); at the start of authoring the working tree was clean, and the memo you are reading plus its snapshot are the one addition this workspace makes to it. TET-28 asks for a refusal: a memo that tells an implementer to modify a symbol must carry a repo-wide census of that symbol, captured by the tool rather than typed, or the memo does not render. The motivating defect, as the ticket reports it from a private project's review trail and described here only by its shape: a memo recommended modifying a function and spent a repo-wide negative about its callers, while the author's actual caller sweep — honest in its own fact — was scoped to a single file; per the ticket, one workspace-rooted grep would have landed on a second production gate with a different mechanism, and the miss instead survived five review rounds before both attack passes found it independently. This memo settles the two questions the ticket leaves open — what the trigger is and whether it is decidable, and what \"workspace-rooted\" means decidably — and reaches a design that keeps the refusal format-level.\n35:What is decidable is a declaration. A modification target becomes a structured record — a symbol string plus a citation of the fact that censuses it — created by a new authoring verb and rendered as its own section, the way the evidence ledger and Facts table already render structured records without generating prose from fields. The refusal then never reads English at all: it fires when a declared target's cited fact carries no qualifying census entry, which is an objective contradiction between the document and the record it claims to rest on — the exact principle the machine partition already states for provenance drift, and the README's own example of the one decidable refusal shape, \"you did not point at your evidence\". The disposition follows the project's report-versus-refuse discriminator from the other side: the note-outside-extent check stayed human-owed because only a person can tell context from conclusion, but for a declared-and-uncensused target nothing is left to tell — the remedy is singular. Run the workspace-rooted grep for that symbol, mint the fact, cite it; or withdraw the target.\n39:Declaration moves the vacuity question to under-declaration, and the honest answer is that no refusal can reach it: deciding that a memo recommended something it never declared is the same heuristic read of prose the trigger just rejected. The design's answer is visibility rather than detection, in two places. First, the section never disappears: once a workspace declares any target the section renders, and once any claim exists it renders even when empty, as \"Modification targets: none declared\" — so a memo full of tell-the-implementer language with an empty census section shows its hole to every reader, instead of an undeclared target being an invisible omission. Second, check's standing non-coverage list — whose first entry is already \"dependents that never declared themselves\" — gains the census's own blind spot, stated as such. The ticket offers a further reason to assess the risk as small: its review protocol's adversarial pass puts every tell-the-implementer claim in scope unconditionally. That is a property of an agent definition outside this repository, not of tetel, so this design records it as the ticket's premise and deliberately does not lean on it — the rendered hole and the printed blind spot carry the visibility argument on their own, for any consumer of the tool, with or without that protocol around it.\n43:The refusal lives at the earliest surface that can decide it: declaration. `tetel target` refuses to create a target whose cited fact has no qualifying census entry — the same authoring-time shape as `tetel fact` refusing on an empty pending buffer. A fact's extent is immutable once minted and a target immutable once declared, so this one refusal makes \"every declared target is censused\" a workspace invariant, and render needs no refusal of its own for workspace-authored documents: its job is to emit the section. What render cannot vouch for, check re-verifies — the rendered document against its own snapshot, reported as [uncensused-target] in the machine partition's scope line — which is what catches a hand-authored or tampered document that never passed through the refusing verb. Like the other snapshot-dependent findings, that check runs only when a snapshot ships; without one, target rows are reported human-owed as unverifiable, consistent with the standing choice that a missing snapshot is human-owed rather than a machine failure. The verb itself must be one dispatch shared by CLI and MCP, the pattern look and fact already use, because this render path has already recorded one CLI/MCP drift on a duplicated act.\n55:check's report already carries a standing non-coverage list, printed item by item, and this feature adds its own blind spots to it rather than leaving them implicit. A textual census cannot see dynamic dispatch, re-exports or aliased imports, or callers assembled by macros and string concatenation. Its traversal is the system grep's — tetel shells out precisely to avoid owning a subtly different regex dialect — so the platform's rules for symlinked directories and the binary-file skip bound what a repo-wide search physically visited. Rooting establishes where the search started, never which tree it should have started in: whether the censused root is the tree the memo designs against remains a reader's question, answerable from the per-root tree-state report the check already prints. Whether anyone read the census is the insincere-examination ceiling: a census is a list of sites, not an understanding of them. And under-declared targets are invisible by construction — the hole in the rendered section and the non-coverage line are their only trace.\n65:The ticket asks that this ship before any TET-27 co-evolution run, on the argument that findings the census would have surfaced must not be double-credited to a prototype, which would make that run's \"would existing machinery have caught this anyway\" kill-arm unevaluable. The premise of that argument lives in the tickets and the private review trail, not in this repository, so this memo does not grade it; what this memo can establish is that the sequencing costs nothing. The feature's surface is one recorded entry in the grep path, two serde-defaulted fields on the extent record, one new record type and verb, one rendered section, one shared refusal, and one machine check with its printed limits — each an extension of a mechanism cited in this memo's facts, and none of them a code-to-graph link, a commit reference, or dependency propagation, the mechanisms the TET-27 ticket describes as its subject. Shipping first is therefore safe whether or not the double-crediting argument is right, and the design was not bent to accommodate it. What this memo deliberately does not decide: any implementation detail beyond the record shapes and predicates above, and any widening of the trigger — in particular, no future measurement of a mention-based advisory should be allowed to harden into a refusal, because the measured evidence in this repository says that detector cannot tell quotation from conclusion.\n90:| C8 | Under-declaration — the memo recommends modifying a symbol it never declared — cannot be a refusal and must not be silently absorbed either. Deciding from prose that a sentence \"tells an implementer to modify X\" is a heuristic read of English, exactly the class the charter routes to the human-owed partition or nowhere; scope.rs's measured precedent shows the symbol-detector that trigger would need flags quotation and misses conclusion. The design therefore makes the omission visible instead of detected: (a) once a workspace declares any target, render emits a Modification targets section, and once any claim exists it emits the section even when empty — \"Modification targets: none declared\" — so a memo that recommends changes while declaring nothing shows a hole where the census should be, in front of every reader; and (b) check's standing non-coverage list, whose first entry is already \"dependents that never declared themselves\", gains a census-specific line (\"a recommendation whose target was never declared\") so the blind spot is printed rather than implicit. | OWED |\n91:| C9 | Where the refusal lives: at declaration, then re-verified at check. The `target` verb refuses to create a target whose cited fact carries no qualifying census entry — the same authoring-time shape as fact refusing on an empty pending buffer (\"a fact needs a preceding look/run\"; a target needs a preceding rooted census). Because a fact's extent is immutable once minted and a target is immutable once declared, this makes \"every declared target is censused\" a workspace invariant, and no render-path refusal is needed for workspace-authored documents; render's job is only to emit the targets section. check independently re-verifies the rendered document against its snapshot and reports [uncensused-target] as a machine failure in the partition's scope line, which is what catches a hand-authored or tampered document that never passed through the refusing verb. Like notes_outside_extent and mint_windows, that check runs only when a snapshot ships; without one, target rows are reported human-owed as unverifiable, consistent with the standing choice that a missing snapshot is human-owed rather than a machine failure. The verb must be one dispatch shared by CLI and MCP, the pattern look and fact already use, because this codebase has recorded a CLI/MCP drift on exactly such a duplicated act. | OWED |\n95:| C13 | The feature's implementation surface is small: one additional recorded entry in look_grep, two serde-defaulted structured fields on the extent entry (following world_root's own compatibility precedent), one new append-only record type whose authoring verb refuses uncensused declarations from one dispatch shared by CLI and MCP, one rendered section beside the existing ledger and Facts tables, and one machine check with its human-owed and non-coverage lines. Each component extends a mechanism this memo's facts describe, and none of them is a code-to-graph link, a commit reference, or dependency propagation — the mechanisms the TET-27 ticket describes as its subject — so shipping this first imposes no design debt on that exploration. | OWED |\n108:| F4 | How a memo is assembled and what render refuses today. compose.rs render(): prose blocks in order, then an appended \"## Evidence ledger\" table (one row per non-withdrawn claim, Status always OWED, no Domain/Extent columns — v1's Domain ⊆ Extent was retired on evidence and deriving scope from facts' extents \"would recreate exactly the vacuity that measurement rejected\"), then a \"## Facts\" table (ID, Note, Extent-as-captured labels, Rests under). Structured tables rendered from fields are the established shape; only labels are rendered, never absolute keys (\"a committed document should not carry /Users/<someone>/...\"). main.rs Render arm (lines 640-700): compose::render, then write document, then snapshot::write (document first so a failed snapshot leaves a recoverable state), then a pending-observations warning that is explicitly \"Warned, never refused: an author may have deliberately looked at something they chose not to cite\". Nothing in the render path refuses on the content of the workspace today. One drift precedent recorded in the comments: the CLI arm used to mint the workspace identity and the MCP render handler did not, so a memo authored over MCP shipped without one — the fix moved the act into snapshot::write, the one shared place. | /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/compose.rs; /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/main.rs lines 640-700 | C9, C13 |\n112:| F8 | Operational, cited by nothing: created the output directory docs/design/ in the tetel repo (mkdir -p, exit 0, no output) so render --out has somewhere to write this memo. Establishes nothing about the design; minted only so the act does not sit in the pending buffer being reported as an unminted observation on every render. | proc: mkdir -p /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/docs/design (exit 0) | — |\n21:{\"_type\":\"https://in-toto.io/Statement/v1\",\"subject\":[{\"name\":\"C13\",\"digest\":{\"sha256\":\"f4b6e64e51c856963b7bb019db7ec1efa12fcb39cbc497509fcf07872a2408e2\"}}],\"predicateType\":\"https://github.com/lonic-software/tetel/captured-fact/v1\",\"predicate\":{\"verdict\":\"qualifies\",\"pass\":\"4789a6bcb5766c0b\",\"reported_kind\":\"run\",\"source\":\"proc:workspace-4789a6bcb5766c0b\",\"extent\":[\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/facts.rs\"],\"world\":[{\"root\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel\",\"state\":\"git:7c8965b105b644cbdac2f4f631373fd31ab2e7f8805fa51cb26d60e9746c7db2\"}],\"note\":\"The precedents the surface leans on are real (world_root's #[serde(default)] compatibility precedent, the shared-dispatch pattern, the rendered ledger and facts table, the snapshot shipping facts.jsonl), and nothing in source contradicts the enumerated component list. But two clauses are not establishable from what I was given: \\\"depends on nothing beyond what this memo's facts describe\\\" quantifies over the author's own facts, which are withheld from this pass by design; and the TET-27 characterization (\\\"no code-to-graph links, no commit references, no dependency propagation\\\") describes a ticket that lives outside this code repository, which this pass may not open. The smallness-of-surface judgment is a forecast, not a property of the pinned tree.\",\"pin\":\"sha256:1121eb1fde8b977582f43b01cc36cdbbfc01bfb881abb0493f23dfe936c0db55\",\"timestamp\":1786132357}}\n35:{\"_type\":\"https://in-toto.io/Statement/v1\",\"subject\":[{\"name\":\"C8\",\"digest\":{\"sha256\":\"a3cda837150f9fa992980a9e5e0dcab8e86dc3912bd1810f8bd0532521f8115e\"}}],\"predicateType\":\"https://github.com/lonic-software/tetel/captured-fact/v1\",\"predicate\":{\"verdict\":\"supports\",\"pass\":\"2a166dfea0f07434\",\"reported_kind\":\"run\",\"source\":\"proc:workspace-2a166dfea0f07434\",\"extent\":[\"proc: grep -rn -e NON_COVERAGE -e never declared /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src (exit 0)\",\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/report.rs lines 1-60\"],\"world\":[{\"root\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel\",\"state\":\"git:7c8965b105b644cbdac2f4f631373fd31ab2e7f8805fa51cb26d60e9746c7db2\"}],\"note\":\"The claim's factual anchor holds: check's standing non-coverage list exists as report.rs's NON_COVERAGE const and its first entry is byte-for-byte \\\"dependents that never declared themselves\\\", so adding a census-specific line extends an existing printed mechanism. The rest of the claim (render section, \\\"none declared\\\" placeholder) is the design being proposed, resting on the premise that detecting recommendations in prose would be a heuristic read of English.\",\"pin\":\"sha256:7a1a7947835bbe0c336926261f24c4a47bd8cf4084922099af0205a073f93b62\",\"timestamp\":1786132937}}\n58:{\"_type\":\"https://in-toto.io/Statement/v1\",\"subject\":[{\"name\":\"C5\",\"digest\":{\"sha256\":\"4570f2d0ffa5c42e1a8b1842c6fb83f53b7c43d32c519d2f9edeeb4a7f3803b3\"}}],\"predicateType\":\"https://github.com/lonic-software/tetel/captured-fact/v1\",\"predicate\":{\"verdict\":\"supports\",\"pass\":\"c1fa7b4466097460\",\"reported_kind\":\"run\",\"source\":\"proc:workspace-c1fa7b4466097460\",\"extent\":[\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/snapshot.rs lines 1-140\"],\"world\":[{\"root\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel\",\"state\":\"git:7c8965b105b644cbdac2f4f631373fd31ab2e7f8805fa51cb26d60e9746c7db2\"}],\"note\":\"The snapshot ships facts.jsonl among its enumerated SNAPSHOT_FILES, and the rendered document itself never carries a fact's extent — so a check reading the proposed structured fields would read them from files every snapshot already contains, as the claim says.\",\"pin\":\"sha256:0df202e8100cd632c3c04fd3d9aef8e235abd8887abc3f77575ffe605f7fad4b\",\"timestamp\":1786133891}}\n74:{\"_type\":\"https://in-toto.io/Statement/v1\",\"subject\":[{\"name\":\"C13\",\"digest\":{\"sha256\":\"65ec3111462b505e233c71d322b17f7471910d2db4c01d383a94c7a20844d878\"}}],\"predicateType\":\"https://github.com/lonic-software/tetel/captured-fact/v1\",\"predicate\":{\"verdict\":\"qualifies\",\"pass\":\"c1fa7b4466097460\",\"reported_kind\":\"run\",\"source\":\"proc:workspace-c1fa7b4466097460\",\"extent\":[\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/checks.rs (grep: objective contradictions)\",\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/checks.rs lines 1-320\",\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/report.rs (grep: NON_COVERAGE)\",\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/report.rs lines 1-80\"],\"world\":[{\"root\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel\",\"state\":\"git:7c8965b105b644cbdac2f4f631373fd31ab2e7f8805fa51cb26d60e9746c7db2\"}],\"note\":\"The verifiable half holds: each listed component extends a mechanism I read in source (look_grep's recorded entries, the serde-defaulted world_root precedent on ExtentEntry, the claim verb's authoring-refusal shape, the shared CLI/MCP dispatch, the rendered ledger/Facts tables, and check's machine/human-owed/non-coverage structure), and none of the listed components is itself a code-to-graph link, a commit reference, or dependency propagation. What I could not establish is the clause characterizing those three as \\\"the mechanisms the TET-27 ticket describes as its subject\\\": the TET-27 ticket is not in this repository, and reading it would leave the code repository this pass is bounded to. The no-design-debt conclusion rests on that unverified characterization.\",\"pin\":\"sha256:2458561f0b6cf8055d71f7036cf970edb6ed55efaa4e5c08b439804e6e7f1239\",\"timestamp\":1786134028}}\n8:{\"event\":\"Create\",\"id\":\"C8\",\"prop\":\"Under-declaration — the memo recommends modifying a symbol it never declared — cannot be a refusal and must not be silently absorbed either. Deciding from prose that a sentence \\\"tells an implementer to modify X\\\" is a heuristic read of English, exactly the class the charter routes to the human-owed partition or nowhere; scope.rs's measured precedent shows the symbol-detector that trigger would need flags quotation and misses conclusion. The design therefore makes the omission visible instead of detected: (a) once a workspace declares any target, render emits a Modification targets section, and once any claim exists it emits the section even when empty — \\\"Modification targets: none declared\\\" — so a memo that recommends changes while declaring nothing shows a hole where the census should be, in front of every reader; and (b) check's standing non-coverage list, whose first entry is already \\\"dependents that never declared themselves\\\", gains a census-specific line (\\\"a recommendation whose target was never declared\\\") so the blind spot is printed rather than implicit.\",\"from\":[\"F5\"],\"timestamp\":1786131213}\n9:{\"event\":\"Create\",\"id\":\"C9\",\"prop\":\"Where the refusal lives, in both surfaces that can see it. (1) render --out refuses — the first content-conditional refusal in the render path, which today only warns (its pending-observations warning is deliberately \\\"warned, never refused\\\" because only the author can tell deliberate from forgotten; an uncensused declared target has no such honest reading). The refusal must live in one shared dispatch used by both CLI and MCP, because the render path has already grown a CLI/MCP drift once — workspace identity was minted by the CLI arm and not the MCP handler until the act moved into snapshot::write, the one shared place. (2) check re-verifies the rendered document against its snapshot as a machine failure, [uncensused-target], joining the partition's scope line; like notes_outside_extent and mint_windows it is computable only when a snapshot ships, so with no snapshot the target rows are reported human-owed as unverifiable, consistent with the existing deliberate choice that a missing snapshot is human-owed rather than a machine failure.\",\"from\":[\"F4\",\"F5\"],\"timestamp\":1786131223}\n13:{\"event\":\"Create\",\"id\":\"C13\",\"prop\":\"The feature's implementation surface is small and depends on nothing beyond what this memo's facts describe: one additional recorded entry in look_grep, two serde-defaulted structured fields on the extent entry (following world_root's own compatibility precedent), one new append-only record type with an authoring verb, one rendered section beside the existing ledger and Facts tables, one refusal in a shared render dispatch, and one machine check with its human-owed and non-coverage lines. In particular it requires no TET-27 mechanism — no code-to-graph links, no commit references, no dependency propagation — so shipping it first imposes no design debt on that exploration.\",\"from\":[\"F2\",\"F3\",\"F4\",\"F5\"],\"timestamp\":1786131411}\n14:{\"event\":\"Revise\",\"id\":\"C9\",\"prop\":\"Where the refusal lives: at declaration, then re-verified at check. The `target` verb refuses to create a target whose cited fact carries no qualifying census entry — the same authoring-time shape as fact refusing on an empty pending buffer (\\\"a fact needs a preceding look/run\\\"; a target needs a preceding rooted census). Because a fact's extent is immutable once minted and a target is immutable once declared, this makes \\\"every declared target is censused\\\" a workspace invariant, and no render-path refusal is needed for workspace-authored documents; render's job is only to emit the targets section. check independently re-verifies the rendered document against its snapshot and reports [uncensused-target] as a machine failure in the partition's scope line, which is what catches a hand-authored or tampered document that never passed through the refusing verb. Like notes_outside_extent and mint_windows, that check runs only when a snapshot ships; without one, target rows are reported human-owed as unverifiable, consistent with the standing choice that a missing snapshot is human-owed rather than a machine failure. The verb must be one dispatch shared by CLI and MCP, the pattern look and fact already use, because this codebase has recorded a CLI/MCP drift on exactly such a duplicated act.\",\"from\":[\"F3\",\"F4\",\"F5\"],\"why\":\"F3 shows the stronger precedent this claim under-used: fact's own empty-buffer refusal establishes authoring-time refusal as the house shape, and applying the census predicate at target creation makes \\\"declared implies censused\\\" a workspace invariant — facts' extents and targets are both immutable — so the render-path refusal in the earlier wording becomes dead code guarding an unreachable state. The check-side verification and the missing-snapshot disposition are unchanged.\",\"timestamp\":1786131525}\n15:{\"event\":\"Revise\",\"id\":\"C13\",\"prop\":\"The feature's implementation surface is small and depends on nothing beyond what this memo's facts describe: one additional recorded entry in look_grep, two serde-defaulted structured fields on the extent entry (following world_root's own compatibility precedent), one new append-only record type whose authoring verb refuses uncensused declarations from one dispatch shared by CLI and MCP, one rendered section beside the existing ledger and Facts tables, and one machine check with its human-owed and non-coverage lines. In particular it requires no TET-27 mechanism — no code-to-graph links, no commit references, no dependency propagation — so shipping it first imposes no design debt on that exploration.\",\"from\":null,\"why\":\"C9's revision moved the refusal from the render dispatch to the target verb's own shared dispatch; this claim's surface list still named the render dispatch and would have contradicted it.\",\"timestamp\":1786131548}\n17:{\"event\":\"Revise\",\"id\":\"C1\",\"prop\":\"This design was built against the tetel repository at commit 57bd0e29a6c8bd3861f3982bebe3486f80e0453d; at the start of authoring, before this memo and its snapshot were themselves written into docs/design/, git status --porcelain printed nothing. Every code citation below refers to that commit; the memo's own output files are the one addition to the tree this workspace makes.\",\"from\":null,\"why\":\"Grounding pass k1 qualified this: \\\"clean working tree\\\" holds only up to the memo's own untracked artifacts, which exist once the memo is rendered into the repo. F1's observation was taken before they existed; the claim now states its own timing instead of asserting a tree state the act of rendering necessarily ends.\",\"timestamp\":1786132443}\n18:{\"event\":\"Revise\",\"id\":\"C13\",\"prop\":\"The feature's implementation surface is small: one additional recorded entry in look_grep, two serde-defaulted structured fields on the extent entry (following world_root's own compatibility precedent), one new append-only record type whose authoring verb refuses uncensused declarations from one dispatch shared by CLI and MCP, one rendered section beside the existing ledger and Facts tables, and one machine check with its human-owed and non-coverage lines. Each component extends a mechanism this memo's facts describe, and none of them is a code-to-graph link, a commit reference, or dependency propagation — the mechanisms the TET-27 ticket describes as its subject — so shipping this first imposes no design debt on that exploration.\",\"from\":null,\"why\":\"Grounding pass k1 qualified two clauses: \\\"depends on nothing beyond what this memo's facts describe\\\" quantifies over facts the grounder cannot see, and the TET-27 characterization describes material outside this repository. Narrowed to what the component list itself shows, with TET-27's subject matter attributed to the ticket that describes it.\",\"timestamp\":1786132452}\n3:{\"event\":\"Create\",\"id\":\"F3\",\"note\":\"What survives from an observation into a fact and its snapshot. pending.rs: PendingEntry carries kind (ObservationKind: Path | GrepMatch | NoMatch | Proc), key, label, output, world_root, world_state, captured_at. facts.rs mint(): the fold copies ONLY key, label, world_root, world_state into ExtentEntry — the kind is dropped, and a grep's pattern exists nowhere except inside the display label text (\\\"no-match: <pattern> in <root>\\\", \\\"<file> (grep: <pattern>)\\\"). Two schema-evolution precedents matter for extending this: (a) world_root is #[serde(default)] for entries minted before it existed, and an empty root is deliberately read as \\\"comparable with nothing\\\" — \\\"check treats it that way rather than guessing\\\"; (b) facts.jsonl ships in the snapshot with full extent entries (mint_windows reads the snapshot's facts.jsonl, and its doc says the Create timestamp \\\"ships in facts.jsonl, and the snapshot copies refusals.log beside it\\\"). Also: there is deliberately no flag on fact's surface that lets a caller supply extent or captured output — \\\"that absence is the guarantee that a fact was actually backed by a look/run\\\". Extent, output and pin are set once at mint and no revision event can carry them.\",\"extent\":[{\"key\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/pending.rs\",\"label\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/pending.rs\",\"world_root\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel\",\"world_state\":\"git:7c8965b105b644cbdac2f4f631373fd31ab2e7f8805fa51cb26d60e9746c7db2\"},{\"key\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/facts.rs\",\"label\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/facts.rs\",\"world_root\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel\",\"world_state\":\"git:7c8965b105b644cbdac2f4f631373fd31ab2e7f8805fa51cb26d60e9746c7db2\"}],\"output\":\"//! The pending observation buffer: what `tetel look`/`tetel run` fill,\\n//! and what `tetel fact` mints from. Persisted as a single JSON file per\\n//! workspace so it survives between separate `tetel` invocations (each\\n//! subcommand is its own process) — but it is ephemeral state, not part\\n//! of the append-only record: it is cleared the moment a fact is\\n//! minted, and never itself gets a log entry.\\n\\nuse std::fs;\\nuse std::io;\\nuse std::path::{Path, PathBuf};\\n\\nuse serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};\\n\\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, Serialize, Deserialize, PartialEq, Eq)]\\npub enum ObservationKind {\\n    Path,\\n    GrepMatch,\\n    NoMatch,\\n    Proc,\\n}\\n\\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\\npub struct PendingEntry {\\n    pub kind: ObservationKind,\\n    /// The overlap/dependency key — a resolved filesystem path when the\\n    /// designator names a file, or a literal command line when it\\n    /// doesn't. Fix 2 in the design memo: the prototype keyed every\\n    /// observation on the literal command string, so three different\\n    /// `sed -n` ranges of one file never overlapped each other or a\\n    /// plain read of it. `tetel look` (with or without `--lines`) and\\n    /// `tetel look --grep`'s per-file matches all key on the resolved\\n    /// path instead, so any two observations of the same file overlap\\n    /// regardless of what range or pattern produced them. `tetel run`\\n    /// stays keyed on its command line — a command names no single file\\n    /// in general, so it remains the one genuinely opaque case.\\n    pub key: String,\\n    /// The human-readable line shown in a fact's extent list.\\n    pub label: String,\\n    pub output: String,\\n    /// The working tree this observation was taken against, and the state\\n    /// it was in — see `worldstate.rs` and fix 1 in the design memo. Both\\n    /// halves are needed: comparing states across two different roots\\n    /// reports a difference that means nothing, and half the workspaces in\\n    /// the store read more than one repository.\\n    ///\\n    /// `world_root` is defaulted for entries written before it existed.\\n    /// Those carry a state resolved from the process's working directory\\n    /// rather than from what they read, so they are not comparable with\\n    /// anything and an empty root is what says so.\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub world_root: String,\\n    pub world_state: String,\\n    /// Unix seconds at capture.\\n    ///\\n    /// Exists so `tetel fact` can say how old what it is folding is. A\\n    /// revision does not clear the buffer and a failed `look` does not\\n    /// add to it, so a `fact` can silently fold an observation from an\\n    /// earlier line of enquiry — which happened, producing a fact whose\\n    /// note described two files its extent never covered. Defaulted for\\n    /// entries written before this field existed.\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub captured_at: u64,\\n}\\n\\nfn path(workspace_dir: &Path) -> PathBuf {\\n    workspace_dir.join(\\\"pending.json\\\")\\n}\\n\\npub fn load(workspace_dir: &Path) -> io::Result<Vec<PendingEntry>> {\\n    let p = path(workspace_dir);\\n    match fs::read_to_string(&p) {\\n        Ok(s) if !s.trim().is_empty() => {\\n            serde_json::from_str(&s).map_err(|e| io::Error::new(io::ErrorKind::InvalidData, e))\\n        }\\n        Ok(_) => Ok(Vec::new()),\\n        Err(e) if e.kind() == io::ErrorKind::NotFound => Ok(Vec::new()),\\n        Err(e) => Err(e),\\n    }\\n}\\n\\npub fn save(workspace_dir: &Path, entries: &[PendingEntry]) -> io::Result<()> {\\n    fs::create_dir_all(workspace_dir)?;\\n    let json = serde_json::to_string_pretty(entries).map_err(|e| io::Error::new(io::ErrorKind::InvalidData, e))?;\\n    fs::write(path(workspace_dir), json)\\n}\\n\\npub fn clear(workspace_dir: &Path) -> io::Result<()> {\\n    save(workspace_dir, &[])\\n}\\n\\n//! `tetel fact` — mints a fact from the pending observation buffer (see\\n//! `pending.rs`). Refuses on an empty buffer. There is deliberately no\\n//! flag anywhere on this module's CLI surface that lets a caller supply\\n//! the extent or captured output themselves — that absence is the\\n//! guarantee that a fact was actually backed by a `look`/`run`, not a\\n//! rule enforced after the fact.\\n//!\\n//! `tetel fact --revise <id> --why <text>` rewrites the note only,\\n//! append-only: extent, output and pin are set once at mint time and\\n//! never appear in a revision event at all, so there is no code path\\n//! that could change them later either.\\n\\nuse std::io;\\nuse std::path::{Path, PathBuf};\\n\\nuse serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};\\n\\nuse crate::evidence::sha256_hex;\\nuse crate::pending;\\nuse crate::workspace::{self, AuthoringError, Kind};\\n\\n/// One observation folded into a fact's extent — the label a human\\n/// reads, the key overlap detection compares, and the world-tree marker\\n/// it was captured under (fix 1 in the design memo).\\n///\\n/// The marker is two fields, not one. `world_state` alone cannot be\\n/// compared across observations of different repositories, and half the\\n/// workspaces in the store on 2026-08-07 read more than one; only\\n/// \\\"same `world_root`, different `world_state`\\\" is a finding. See\\n/// `worldstate.rs`.\\n///\\n/// `world_root` is `#[serde(default)]` for entries minted before it\\n/// existed. An empty root is not \\\"unknown repository\\\" — it marks a state\\n/// that was resolved from the process's working directory rather than\\n/// from what the observation touched, and is therefore comparable with\\n/// nothing. `check` treats it that way rather than guessing.\\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\\npub struct ExtentEntry {\\n    pub key: String,\\n    pub label: String,\\n    #[serde(default)]\\n    pub world_root: String,\\n    pub world_state: String,\\n}\\n\\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\\n#[serde(tag = \\\"event\\\")]\\npub enum FactEvent {\\n    Create {\\n        id: String,\\n        note: String,\\n        extent: Vec<ExtentEntry>,\\n        output: String,\\n        pin: String,\\n        timestamp: u64,\\n    },\\n    /// Append-only note revision. No `extent`, `output` or `pin` field\\n    /// exists on this variant at all — see the module doc comment.\\n    Revise {\\n        id: String,\\n        note: String,\\n        why: String,\\n        timestamp: u64,\\n    },\\n}\\n\\n/// A fact's current view, replayed from its event log: the original\\n/// extent/output/pin (never revisable) plus whichever note is current.\\npub struct Fact {\\n    pub id: String,\\n    pub note: String,\\n    pub extent: Vec<ExtentEntry>,\\n    pub output: String,\\n    pub pin: String,\\n    pub revisions: usize,\\n}\\n\\nfn log_path(workspace_dir: &Path) -> PathBuf {\\n    workspace_dir.join(\\\"facts.jsonl\\\")\\n}\\n\\npub fn load_all(workspace_dir: &Path) -> io::Result<Vec<Fact>> {\\n    let events: Vec<FactEvent> = workspace::read_jsonl(&log_path(workspace_dir))?;\\n    let mut by_id: std::collections::BTreeMap<String, Fact> = std::collections::BTreeMap::new();\\n    let mut order: Vec<String> = Vec::new();\\n    for ev in events {\\n        match ev {\\n            FactEvent::Create { id, note, extent, output, pin, .. } => {\\n                order.push(id.clone());\\n                by_id.insert(id.clone(), Fact { id, note, extent, output, pin, revisions: 0 });\\n            }\\n            FactEvent::Revise { id, note, .. } => {\\n                if let Some(f) = by_id.get_mut(&id) {\\n                    f.note = note;\\n                    f.revisions += 1;\\n                }\\n            }\\n        }\\n    }\\n    Ok(order.into_iter().filter_map(|id| by_id.remove(&id)).collect())\\n}\\n\\npub fn exists(workspace_dir: &Path, id: &str) -> io::Result<bool> {\\n    Ok(load_all(workspace_dir)?.iter().any(|f| f.id == id))\\n}\\n\\npub fn get(workspace_dir: &Path, id: &str) -> io::Result<Option<Fact>> {\\n    Ok(load_all(workspace_dir)?.into_iter().find(|f| f.id == id))\\n}\\n\\n/// How old each observation in the buffer is, at the moment it is folded.\\n///\\n/// `fact` prints this because a buffer is not necessarily what you just\\n/// looked at. A revision does not clear it and a failed `look` does not\\n/// add to it, so a mint can fold an observation from an earlier line of\\n/// enquiry — which happened, silently, producing a fact whose note\\n/// described two source files its extent never covered.\\n///\\n/// Reported rather than refused, and without a staleness threshold: there\\n/// is no age at which folding an earlier observation becomes wrong, and a\\n/// number chosen here would be arbitrary. What was missing was not a rule\\n/// but the fact being visible at all — an author who expected two file\\n/// reads and is told they folded one four-minute-old command has what\\n/// they need.\\npub fn describe_buffer(entries: &[crate::pending::PendingEntry], now: u64) -> Vec<String> {\\n    entries\\n        .iter()\\n        .map(|e| {\\n            let age = now.saturating_sub(e.captured_at);\\n            let when = if e.captured_at == 0 {\\n                \\\"age unknown\\\".to_string()\\n            } else if age < 60 {\\n                format!(\\\"{age}s ago\\\")\\n            } else {\\n                format!(\\\"{}m ago\\\", age / 60)\\n            };\\n            format!(\\\"{} ({when})\\\", e.label)\\n        })\\n        .collect()\\n}\\n\\n/// When this workspace last minted a fact, or `None` if it never has.\\n///\\n/// Reads the raw event log rather than [`load_all`], which discards\\n/// `Create`'s timestamp when building its in-memory view. The timestamp\\n/// is in the shipped file the whole time; only the view drops it.\\npub fn last_mint_timestamp(workspace_dir: &Path) -> Option<u64> {\\n    let events: Vec<FactEvent> = workspace::read_jsonl(&log_path(workspace_dir)).ok()?;\\n    events\\n        .iter()\\n        .filter_map(|e| match e {\\n            FactEvent::Create { timestamp, .. } => Some(*timestamp),\\n            FactEvent::Revise { .. } => None,\\n        })\\n        .max()\\n}\\n\\n/// Every refusal this workspace recorded since its last mint, verbatim.\\n///\\n/// # Why a mint reports what it could not do\\n///\\n/// [`describe_buffer`] says what a mint *folded*. It cannot say why what\\n/// the author expected to fold is missing — and that gap is the whole\\n/// incident this exists for: two `look` calls were refused for a\\n/// malformed line range, each left the pending buffer untouched, and the\\n/// next mint folded a leftover observation from an earlier line of\\n/// enquiry. The author saw a folding line with an age on it and nothing\\n/// else. The refusals had happened, and nothing the tool ever consults\\n/// had recorded them.\\n///\\n/// So the two halves are complementary and both are needed: what was\\n/// folded, and what was refused in the window that produced it. Neither\\n/// is a refusal itself, neither is counted, and neither carries a\\n/// threshold — a mint following a refusal is often entirely correct.\\n///\\n/// One helper, called by both surfaces, so CLI and MCP cannot drift on\\n/// which refusals a mint reports.\\npub fn refusals_since_last_mint(workspace_dir: &Path) -> Vec<String> {\\n    workspace::refusals_since(workspace_dir, last_mint_timestamp(workspace_dir))\\n}\\n\\n/// One fact's mint window: the refusals recorded between the previous\\n/// mint and this one.\\npub struct MintWindow {\\n    pub fact_id: String,\\n    pub refusals: Vec<String>,\\n    /// The first fact in the workspace, whose window has no previous mint\\n    /// to open at and so runs from the beginning of the log.\\n    pub is_first: bool,\\n    /// At least one of these refusals shares a second with a mint\\n    /// boundary, so it cannot be placed on one side of it. Such a refusal\\n    /// appears in both adjacent windows — the same `>=` choice the\\n    /// mint-time replay makes, for the same reason: showing twice is\\n    /// recoverable, hiding once is the incident this exists for.\\n    pub straddles_a_boundary: bool,\\n}\\n\\n/// Every fact whose mint window contains a refusal, in mint order.\\n///\\n/// # Why this exists beside the mint-time line\\n///\\n/// [`refusals_since_last_mint`] prints at the moment of minting, and that\\n/// is the only moment the defect is *preventable* rather than merely\\n/// discoverable — a fact's extent is unrevisable, so nothing later can\\n/// correct the evidence, only the note.\\n///\\n/// But a line printed at a terminal works only if it is read in the\\n/// moment, and a memo built on a bad fact otherwise renders and checks\\n/// clean. This recovers the same signal at grading time, when nobody is\\n/// relying on the author's attention: `FactEvent::Create` carries a\\n/// timestamp that ships in `facts.jsonl`, and the snapshot copies\\n/// `refusals.log` beside it, so the windows are derivable from files\\n/// every snapshot already contains. No schema change, nothing new stored.\\n///\\n/// Human-owed and verbatim: a mint following a refusal is frequently\\n/// correct, and which of them matters is a reader's call. Nothing here\\n/// counts, scores or ranks.\\n///\\n/// The listing is only ever as complete as the log beneath it — a\\n/// snapshot written by a build that did not record surface-layer\\n/// refusals simply has fewer lines, and this is silent about them. That\\n/// is not a completeness claim in either direction: a verbatim listing\\n/// asserts only what was recorded.\\npub fn mint_windows(snapshot_dir: &Path) -> Vec<MintWindow> {\\n    let Ok(events) = workspace::read_jsonl::<FactEvent>(&log_path(snapshot_dir)) else {\\n        return Vec::new();\\n    };\\n    let mints: Vec<(String, u64)> = events\\n        .iter()\\n        .filter_map(|e| match e {\\n            FactEvent::Create { id, timestamp, .. } => Some((id.clone(), *timestamp)),\\n            FactEvent::Revise { .. } => None,\\n        })\\n        .collect();\\n\\n    let mut out = Vec::new();\\n    for (i, (id, minted_at)) in mints.iter().enumerate() {\\n        // The window opens at the previous mint — or at the beginning of\\n        // the log for the first fact, which owns everything refused\\n        // before it.\\n        let opened = if i == 0 { None } else { Some(mints[i - 1].1) };\\n        let stamped: Vec<(u64, String)> = workspace::refusals_since(snapshot_dir, opened)\\n            .into_iter()\\n            .filter_map(|line| {\\n                let ts = line.split_once('\\\\t').and_then(|(ts, _)| ts.parse::<u64>().ok())?;\\n                (ts <= *minted_at).then_some((ts, line))\\n            })\\n            .collect();\\n        if !stamped.is_empty() {\\n            let straddles_a_boundary = stamped\\n                .iter()\\n                .any(|(ts, _)| *ts == *minted_at || opened.is_some_and(|o| *ts == o));\\n            out.push(MintWindow {\\n                fact_id: id.clone(),\\n                refusals: stamped.into_iter().map(|(_, line)| line).collect(),\\n                is_first: i == 0,\\n                straddles_a_boundary,\\n            });\\n        }\\n    }\\n    out\\n}\\n\\n/// Mint a fact from the current pending buffer. Refuses if the buffer\\n/// is empty (a fact needs a preceding `look`/`run`) or the note text is\\n/// empty. Clears the buffer on success, exactly once, after the log\\n/// append succeeds — never before.\\npub fn mint(workspace_dir: &Path, note: &str) -> Result<Fact, AuthoringError> {\\n    if note.trim().is_empty() {\\n        return Err(workspace::refuse(workspace_dir, \\\"fact\\\", \\\"note text is empty\\\"));\\n    }\\n    let buf = pending::load(workspace_dir)?;\\n    if buf.is_empty() {\\n        return Err(workspace::refuse(\\n            workspace_dir,\\n            \\\"fact\\\",\\n            \\\"pending observation buffer is empty; run `tetel look` or `tetel run` first, then `tetel fact`\\\",\\n        ));\\n    }\\n\\n    let extent: Vec<ExtentEntry> = buf\\n        .iter()\\n        .map(|e| ExtentEntry {\\n            key: e.key.clone(),\\n            label: e.label.clone(),\\n            world_root: e.world_root.clone(),\\n            world_state: e.world_state.clone(),\\n        })\\n        .collect();\\n    let output = buf.iter().filter(|e| !e.output.is_empty()).map(|e| e.output.as_str()).collect::<Vec<_>>().join(\\\"\\\\n\\\");\\n\\n    // The pin: a content fingerprint over every entry's label, output\\n    // and world-tree marker — the prototype's compute_pin hashed\\n    // extent labels and captured output; folding the marker in too means\\n    // a fact minted against a different tree state pins differently even\\n    // if its labels/output happen to coincide. Both halves of the marker\\n    // go in: two observations of the same path under the same state but\\n    // in two different checkouts are two different observations. The\\n    // marker itself is still carried separately in `extent` (see\\n    // `ExtentEntry`), since a hash alone can't be compared by a human\\n    // without recomputing it — fix 1 is about visibility, not just\\n    // detectability.\\n    let mut hash_input = String::new();\\n    for e in &buf {\\n        hash_input.push_str(&e.label);\\n        hash_input.push('\\\\n');\\n        hash_input.push_str(&e.world_root);\\n        hash_input.push('\\\\n');\\n        hash_input.push_str(&e.world_state);\\n        hash_input.push('\\\\n');\\n        hash_input.push_str(&e.output);\\n        hash_input.push('\\\\n');\\n    }\\n    let pin = format!(\\\"sha256:{}\\\", sha256_hex(&hash_input));\\n\\n    let id = workspace::next_id(workspace_dir, Kind::Fact)?;\\n    let event = FactEvent::Create {\\n        id: id.clone(),\\n        note: note.to_string(),\\n        extent: extent.clone(),\\n        output: output.clone(),\\n        pin: pin.clone(),\\n        timestamp: workspace::now_unix(),\\n    };\\n    workspace::append_jsonl(&log_path(workspace_dir), &event)?;\\n    pending::clear(workspace_dir)?;\\n\\n    Ok(Fact { id, note: note.to_string(), extent, output, pin, revisions: 0 })\\n}\\n\\n/// `tetel fact --revise <id> --note <new-text> --why <text>`.\\npub fn revise(workspace_dir: &Path, id: &str, new_note: &str, why: &str) -> Result<(), AuthoringError> {\\n    if !exists(workspace_dir, id)? {\\n        return Err(workspace::refuse(workspace_dir, \\\"fact\\\", format!(\\\"no such fact: {id}\\\")));\\n    }\\n    if why.trim().is_empty() {\\n        return Err(workspace::refuse(workspace_dir, \\\"fact\\\", \\\"--revise requires --why (revisions must explain themselves)\\\"));\\n    }\\n    if new_note.trim().is_empty() {\\n        return Err(workspace::refuse(workspace_dir, \\\"fact\\\", \\\"no new --note text given\\\"));\\n    }\\n    let event =\\n        FactEvent::Revise { id: id.to_string(), note: new_note.to_string(), why: why.to_string(), timestamp: workspace::now_unix() };\\n    workspace::append_jsonl(&log_path(workspace_dir), &event)?;\\n    Ok(())\\n}\\n\\n/// What `tetel fact` was asked to do — mint a new fact from the pending\\n/// buffer, or revise an existing one's note. The single shape both the\\n/// CLI and the MCP server build and pass to [`dispatch`], so the two\\n/// front ends can never drift on which combination of missing flags gets\\n/// refused, or with what text.\\npub enum FactRequest {\\n    Mint { note: Option<String> },\\n    Revise { id: String, note: Option<String>, why: Option<String> },\\n}\\n\\npub enum FactOutcome {\\n    Minted(Fact),\\n    Revised { id: String },\\n}\\n\\n/// Dispatches a [`FactRequest`] to [`mint`] or [`revise`], refusing with\\n/// the exact text the CLI has always printed for a flag a mode required\\n/// but didn't get — lifted here (rather than left as a bare `eprintln!`\\n/// in `main.rs`) so the MCP server's structured refusals carry the same\\n/// guidance the CLI does, from the one place that decides it.\\npub fn dispatch(workspace_dir: &Path, req: FactRequest) -> Result<FactOutcome, AuthoringError> {\\n    match req {\\n        FactRequest::Mint { note } => {\\n            let note = note.ok_or_else(|| workspace::refuse(workspace_dir, \\\"fact\\\", \\\"fact requires --note\\\"))?;\\n            mint(workspace_dir, &note).map(FactOutcome::Minted)\\n        }\\n        FactRequest::Revise { id, note, why } => {\\n            let why = why.ok_or_else(|| workspace::refuse(workspace_dir, \\\"fact\\\", \\\"fact --revise requires --why\\\"))?;\\n            let note = note.ok_or_else(|| {\\n                workspace::refuse(workspace_dir, \\\"fact\\\", \\\"fact --revise requires --note (the new note text)\\\")\\n            })?;\\n            revise(workspace_dir, &id, &note, &why).map(|()| FactOutcome::Revised { id })\\n        }\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n#[cfg(test)]\\nmod window_tests {\\n    use super::*;\\n\\n    fn scratch(tag: &str) -> PathBuf {\\n        let dir = std::env::temp_dir().join(format!(\\n            \\\"tetel-window-{tag}-{}-{}\\\",\\n            std::process::id(),\\n            std::time::SystemTime::now()\\n                .duration_since(std::time::UNIX_EPOCH)\\n                .unwrap()\\n                .as_nanos()\\n        ));\\n        std::fs::create_dir_all(&dir).unwrap();\\n        dir\\n    }\\n\\n    /// Facts as raw `Create` events at chosen timestamps, plus a refusal\\n    /// log at chosen timestamps — the two files a snapshot ships. Written\\n    /// by hand because the whole point is controlling the seconds, which\\n    /// an end-to-end run cannot do.\\n    fn seed(dir: &Path, mints: &[(&str, u64)], refusals: &[(u64, &str)]) {\\n        let facts: String = mints\\n            .iter()\\n            .map(|(id, ts)| {\\n                format!(\\n                    r#\\\"{{\\\"event\\\":\\\"Create\\\",\\\"id\\\":\\\"{id}\\\",\\\"note\\\":\\\"n\\\",\\\"extent\\\":[],\\\"output\\\":\\\"\\\",\\\"pin\\\":\\\"p\\\",\\\"timestamp\\\":{ts}}}\\\"#\\n                ) + \\\"\\\\n\\\"\\n            })\\n            .collect();\\n        std::fs::write(dir.join(\\\"facts.jsonl\\\"), facts).unwrap();\\n        let log: String =\\n            refusals.iter().map(|(ts, r)| format!(\\\"{ts}\\\\tlook\\\\t{r}\\\\n\\\")).collect();\\n        std::fs::write(dir.join(\\\"refusals.log\\\"), log).unwrap();\\n    }\\n\\n    #[test]\\n    fn each_refusal_lands_in_the_window_of_the_mint_that_followed_it() {\\n        let dir = scratch(\\\"attribution\\\");\\n        seed(&dir, &[(\\\"F1\\\", 100), (\\\"F2\\\", 200)], &[(50, \\\"before F1\\\"), (150, \\\"between F1 and F2\\\")]);\\n\\n        let w = mint_windows(&dir);\\n        assert_eq!(w.len(), 2, \\\"both facts have a refusal in their window\\\");\\n        assert_eq!(w[0].fact_id, \\\"F1\\\");\\n        assert!(w[0].is_first, \\\"F1 has no previous mint to open at\\\");\\n        assert!(w[0].refusals[0].contains(\\\"before F1\\\"));\\n        assert_eq!(w[0].refusals.len(), 1, \\\"F1 must not claim a refusal that followed it\\\");\\n        assert_eq!(w[1].fact_id, \\\"F2\\\");\\n        assert!(!w[1].is_first);\\n        assert!(w[1].refusals[0].contains(\\\"between F1 and F2\\\"));\\n        assert_eq!(w[1].refusals.len(), 1);\\n        assert!(!w[0].straddles_a_boundary && !w[1].straddles_a_boundary, \\\"no shared seconds here\\\");\\n\\n        let _ = std::fs::remove_dir_all(&dir);\\n    }\\n\\n    /// A refusal sharing a second with a mint cannot be placed on one\\n    /// side of it. It is listed under both adjacent facts and the report\\n    /// says why, rather than a guess being made silently — the same\\n    /// choice the mint-time replay makes.\\n    #[test]\\n    fn a_refusal_sharing_a_second_with_a_mint_is_flagged_not_guessed() {\\n        let dir = scratch(\\\"straddle\\\");\\n        seed(&dir, &[(\\\"F1\\\", 100), (\\\"F2\\\", 200)], &[(100, \\\"same second as F1\\\")]);\\n\\n        let w = mint_windows(&dir);\\n        assert_eq!(w.len(), 2, \\\"listed under both, since which side it fell on is unknowable\\\");\\n        assert!(w[0].straddles_a_boundary, \\\"F1's window closes at this second\\\");\\n        assert!(w[1].straddles_a_boundary, \\\"F2's window opens at it\\\");\\n\\n        let _ = std::fs::remove_dir_all(&dir);\\n    }\\n\\n    /// A fact with nothing refused in its window is not listed at all. An\\n    /// entry per fact saying \\\"none\\\" is noise, and noise is what makes a\\n    /// real one invisible.\\n    #[test]\\n    fn a_fact_with_a_clean_window_is_absent_rather_than_reported_empty() {\\n        let dir = scratch(\\\"clean\\\");\\n        seed(&dir, &[(\\\"F1\\\", 100), (\\\"F2\\\", 200)], &[(150, \\\"only in F2's window\\\")]);\\n        let w = mint_windows(&dir);\\n        assert_eq!(w.len(), 1);\\n        assert_eq!(w[0].fact_id, \\\"F2\\\");\\n        let _ = std::fs::remove_dir_all(&dir);\\n    }\\n\\n    /// A snapshot from a build that never recorded surface-layer\\n    /// refusals has no log; the listing is silent rather than absent-\\n    /// meaning-clean. A verbatim listing asserts only what was recorded.\\n    #[test]\\n    fn a_snapshot_with_no_refusal_log_yields_no_windows() {\\n        let dir = scratch(\\\"nolog\\\");\\n        seed(&dir, &[(\\\"F1\\\", 100)], &[]);\\n        std::fs::remove_file(dir.join(\\\"refusals.log\\\")).unwrap();\\n        assert!(mint_windows(&dir).is_empty());\\n        let _ = std::fs::remove_dir_all(&dir);\\n    }\\n}\\n\",\"pin\":\"sha256:d7f068e1226cf2a735410e1680d591f77594f6e772529431d8e541bd22d13f38\",\"timestamp\":1786130935}\n4:{\"event\":\"Create\",\"id\":\"F4\",\"note\":\"How a memo is assembled and what render refuses today. compose.rs render(): prose blocks in order, then an appended \\\"## Evidence ledger\\\" table (one row per non-withdrawn claim, Status always OWED, no Domain/Extent columns — v1's Domain ⊆ Extent was retired on evidence and deriving scope from facts' extents \\\"would recreate exactly the vacuity that measurement rejected\\\"), then a \\\"## Facts\\\" table (ID, Note, Extent-as-captured labels, Rests under). Structured tables rendered from fields are the established shape; only labels are rendered, never absolute keys (\\\"a committed document should not carry /Users/<someone>/...\\\"). main.rs Render arm (lines 640-700): compose::render, then write document, then snapshot::write (document first so a failed snapshot leaves a recoverable state), then a pending-observations warning that is explicitly \\\"Warned, never refused: an author may have deliberately looked at something they chose not to cite\\\". Nothing in the render path refuses on the content of the workspace today. One drift precedent recorded in the comments: the CLI arm used to mint the workspace identity and the MCP render handler did not, so a memo authored over MCP shipped without one — the fix moved the act into snapshot::write, the one shared place.\",\"extent\":[{\"key\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/compose.rs\",\"label\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/compose.rs\",\"world_root\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel\",\"world_state\":\"git:7c8965b105b644cbdac2f4f631373fd31ab2e7f8805fa51cb26d60e9746c7db2\"},{\"key\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/main.rs\",\"label\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/main.rs lines 640-700\",\"world_root\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel\",\"world_state\":\"git:7c8965b105b644cbdac2f4f631373fd31ab2e7f8805fa51cb26d60e9746c7db2\"}],\"output\":\"//! `tetel render` — assemble the workspace's current prose state into\\n//! markdown on stdout, followed by an evidence ledger built from the\\n//! workspace's claims. Mirrors `trender`, plus fix 1 from this port's own\\n//! design memo: the prototype's `trender` had nothing downstream that\\n//! read its output back, so a rendered document and `tetel check` never\\n//! connected — every check reported \\\"no evidence ledger found\\\" against\\n//! a document this same tool had just produced. Appending a ledger\\n//! table in the shape `ledger::import` already reads closes that loop\\n//! without inventing a third evidence shape.\\n//!\\n//! Headings render at their own declared depth (`#` * level), not a\\n//! single fixed depth — see `prose.rs`'s doc comment on why that's a\\n//! fix, not a stylistic choice. A revised block renders only its\\n//! current text; superseded text lives in `prose.jsonl`'s history but\\n//! never here.\\n//!\\n//! The ledger is strictly an addition after the prose: nothing here can\\n//! change a single byte the prose loop already wrote, so the document a\\n//! human reads stays exactly what was authored — only `tetel check`\\n//! sees the appended table as evidence to grade.\\n\\nuse std::io;\\nuse std::path::Path;\\n\\nuse crate::claims;\\nuse crate::prose;\\n\\npub fn render(workspace_dir: &Path) -> io::Result<String> {\\n    let blocks = prose::load_all(workspace_dir)?;\\n    let mut out = String::new();\\n    for (i, b) in blocks.iter().enumerate() {\\n        if i > 0 {\\n            out.push('\\\\n');\\n        }\\n        if b.heading {\\n            let level = b.level.unwrap_or(2).clamp(1, 6);\\n            out.push_str(&\\\"#\\\".repeat(level as usize));\\n            out.push(' ');\\n            out.push_str(&b.text);\\n            out.push('\\\\n');\\n        } else {\\n            out.push_str(&b.text);\\n            out.push('\\\\n');\\n            if !b.cite.is_empty() {\\n                out.push('\\\\n');\\n                out.push_str(&format!(\\\"*cites: {}*\\\\n\\\", b.cite.join(\\\", \\\")));\\n            }\\n        }\\n    }\\n    let claim_list = claims::load_all(workspace_dir)?;\\n    out.push_str(&render_ledger(&claim_list));\\n    out.push_str(&render_facts(\\n        &crate::facts::load_all(workspace_dir)?,\\n        &claim_list,\\n    ));\\n    Ok(out)\\n}\\n\\n/// Appends a facts table after the evidence ledger.\\n///\\n/// # Why the document has to carry this\\n///\\n/// Before it did, `prose --cites` accepted fact ids as readily as claim\\n/// ids and `render` wrote them into the document, but no fact appeared\\n/// anywhere in the output — so `tetel check` reported `cited but\\n/// undefined: [F5]` on the first real memo, correctly, and would have on\\n/// every memo that ever cited a fact from prose.\\n///\\n/// The deeper reason is what a reader loses without it. A fact's captured\\n/// extent is the one field in this system no author can type, and reading\\n/// notes against extents is where review value has actually shown up —\\n/// the FORK-94 review's sharpest finding was a note concluding about a\\n/// file its extent never covered. Without this table that comparison is\\n/// possible only by opening the workspace, which a reviewer of a\\n/// committed document does not have.\\n///\\n/// # What is deliberately not here\\n///\\n/// The `key` is an absolute machine-local path; the `label` is\\n/// repo-relative. Only the label is rendered — a committed document\\n/// should not carry `/Users/<someone>/...`, and the label is what a\\n/// reader can act on anyway.\\n///\\n/// Captured output is not rendered either. It is unbounded (a `run` can\\n/// capture megabytes), it is in the snapshot for anyone who needs it, and\\n/// a document that inlined it would bury the prose it exists to support.\\nfn render_facts(facts: &[crate::facts::Fact], claims: &[claims::Claim]) -> String {\\n    if facts.is_empty() {\\n        return String::new();\\n    }\\n    // Which claims rest on each fact — so a reader landing on `F5` from\\n    // prose can see what was built on it without scanning every row.\\n    let cited_by = |id: &str| -> String {\\n        let names: Vec<&str> = claims\\n            .iter()\\n            .filter(|c| !c.withdrawn && c.from.iter().any(|f| f == id))\\n            .map(|c| c.id.as_str())\\n            .collect();\\n        if names.is_empty() {\\n            \\\"—\\\".to_string()\\n        } else {\\n            names.join(\\\", \\\")\\n        }\\n    };\\n\\n    let mut out = String::new();\\n    out.push_str(\\\"\\\\n## Facts\\\\n\\\\n\\\");\\n    out.push_str(\\n        \\\"Observations recorded with `tetel look`/`tetel run`, then minted with `tetel fact`. \\\\\\nExtent is captured by the tool and cannot be typed by an author; the note is written. Where \\\\\\na note names a location the extent does not cover, `tetel check` says so — reading those two \\\\\\ncolumns against each other is the point of this table.\\\\n\\\\n\\\",\\n    );\\n    out.push_str(\\\"| ID | Note | Extent (captured) | Rests under |\\\\n\\\");\\n    out.push_str(\\\"|---|---|---|---|\\\\n\\\");\\n    for f in facts {\\n        let extent = f\\n            .extent\\n            .iter()\\n            .map(|e| e.label.as_str())\\n            .collect::<Vec<_>>()\\n            .join(\\\"; \\\");\\n        out.push_str(&format!(\\n            \\\"| {} | {} | {} | {} |\\\\n\\\",\\n            ledger_cell(&f.id),\\n            ledger_cell(&f.note),\\n            ledger_cell(&extent),\\n            cited_by(&f.id),\\n        ));\\n    }\\n    out\\n}\\n\\n/// Escape a claim proposition for safe embedding in one evidence-ledger\\n/// table cell: a bare `|` would otherwise be read as a cell separator\\n/// (see `ledger::split_row_cells`), and an embedded newline would end\\n/// the table row early, since every row of a markdown table is exactly\\n/// one physical line. A plain backslash is left alone — the ledger\\n/// importer only treats a backslash specially when it precedes `|`, so\\n/// an unrelated `\\\\` round-trips unchanged.\\nfn ledger_cell(text: &str) -> String {\\n    text.replace('|', \\\"\\\\\\\\|\\\").replace('\\\\n', \\\" \\\")\\n}\\n\\n/// Appends, after the prose, one evidence-ledger row per non-withdrawn\\n/// claim — the shape `ledger::import` already reads (see its module doc\\n/// comment), reused rather than a third shape invented for this. Empty\\n/// when there are no claims: a document with nothing to cite has nothing\\n/// to check either.\\n///\\n/// There are no Domain/Extent columns. A claim minted by `tetel claim`\\n/// (see `claims.rs`) has no scope field of its own, because v1's\\n/// `Domain ⊆ Extent` was retired on evidence — and deriving one from its\\n/// facts' extents would recreate exactly the vacuity that measurement\\n/// rejected: a field and its own check answered by one act.\\n///\\n/// Earlier this table carried the two columns anyway, filled with a\\n/// sentinel, which put two permanently unfillable cells in front of every\\n/// reader and made `check` print one undischargeable line per claim. The\\n/// absence is now stated once, in the paragraph above the table and once\\n/// in `check`'s human-owed list. What a claim rests on lives in the Facts\\n/// table, where the extent beside it was captured rather than typed.\\n///\\n/// Status is always `OWED`: a claim just authored has not been\\n/// independently graded by anything this crate can see yet (that is\\n/// what `tetel record` is for), and never `VERIFIED`/`REFUTED` — either\\n/// would assert a verdict nobody gave.\\nfn render_ledger(claims: &[claims::Claim]) -> String {\\n    let rows: Vec<&claims::Claim> = claims.iter().filter(|c| !c.withdrawn).collect();\\n    if rows.is_empty() {\\n        return String::new();\\n    }\\n    let mut out = String::new();\\n    out.push_str(\\\"\\\\n## Evidence ledger\\\\n\\\\n\\\");\\n    out.push_str(\\n        \\\"Claims recorded with `tetel claim`. No claim here declares a scope — the authoring \\\\\\nmodel has no such field, and no coverage claim of any strength is made for any row. What each \\\\\\nclaim rests on is in the Facts table below.\\\\n\\\\n\\\",\\n    );\\n    out.push_str(\\\"| ID | Proposition | Status |\\\\n\\\");\\n    out.push_str(\\\"|---|---|---|\\\\n\\\");\\n    for c in rows {\\n        out.push_str(&format!(\\n            \\\"| {} | {} | OWED |\\\\n\\\",\\n            ledger_cell(&c.id),\\n            ledger_cell(&c.prop),\\n        ));\\n    }\\n    out\\n}\\n\\n                    ExitCode::from(0)\\n                }\\n                Err(e) => {\\n                    eprintln!(\\\"tetel: {e}\\\");\\n                    ExitCode::from(1)\\n                }\\n            }\\n        }\\n        Command::Render { out } => {\\n            let workspace_dir = tetel::workspace::workspace_dir(&cli.workspace);\\n            let rendered = match tetel::compose::render(&workspace_dir) {\\n                Ok(r) => r,\\n                Err(e) => {\\n                    eprintln!(\\\"tetel: error rendering: {e}\\\");\\n                    return ExitCode::from(1);\\n                }\\n            };\\n            let Some(path) = out else {\\n                print!(\\\"{rendered}\\\");\\n                return ExitCode::from(0);\\n            };\\n\\n            // Document first, then snapshot: if the snapshot write fails\\n            // the document still exists and `check` reports the missing\\n            // record, which is a recoverable state. The reverse order\\n            // could leave a snapshot claiming to describe a document that\\n            // was never written.\\n            if let Err(e) = std::fs::write(&path, &rendered) {\\n                eprintln!(\\\"tetel: could not write {}: {e}\\\", path.display());\\n                return ExitCode::from(1);\\n            }\\n            // The workspace identity the snapshot needs is minted by\\n            // `snapshot::write` itself — deliberately not here. This arm\\n            // used to do it and the MCP render handler did not, so a memo\\n            // authored over MCP shipped without one; see that function's\\n            // doc comment.\\n            if let Err(e) = tetel::snapshot::write(&path, &workspace_dir) {\\n                eprintln!(\\n                    \\\"tetel: wrote {} but could not write its snapshot: {e}\\\",\\n                    path.display()\\n                );\\n                return ExitCode::from(1);\\n            }\\n\\n            // Warned, never refused: an author may have deliberately\\n            // looked at something they chose not to cite, and only they\\n            // can tell that from having forgotten to mint it.\\n            let pending = tetel::snapshot::pending_count(&workspace_dir);\\n            if pending > 0 {\\n                eprintln!(\\n                    \\\"tetel: warning: {pending} observation(s) still pending, never minted into a \\\\\\nfact — they are in the snapshot but nothing in the document rests on them\\\"\\n                );\\n            }\\n            println!(\\n                \\\"{} written, snapshot in {}\\\",\\n                path.display(),\\n                tetel::snapshot::snapshot_path(&path).display()\\n            );\\n            ExitCode::from(0)\\n        }\",\"pin\":\"sha256:11cf63ef2d447eb0357101e15e24c112c42654aa7ef227a3740cf400b1b11a69\",\"timestamp\":1786131087}\n5:{\"event\":\"Create\",\"id\":\"F5\",\"note\":\"The project's refusal doctrine and where TET-28 already sits in the roadmap. README Constraints: \\\"Refusals are format-level, never heuristic. 'You did not point at your evidence' is a decidable question. 'This looks wrong' is not, and belongs in the human-owed partition or nowhere.\\\" Two partitions, never a single document-level verdict; machine-checked failures are the grammar/subset/abutting/unsettled/cascade/ledger/verdict/out-of-proof/provenance-drift set; human-owed \\\"none of it is settled by a passing check, and none of it fails the run\\\". README Direction already lists, gated on measurement: \\\"More authoring-time refusals, where they are decidable. Requiring a workspace-rooted search behind a CLAIM THAT NAMES A SYMBOL\\\" — i.e. the roadmap phrasing is mention-triggered — while the same section warns that \\\"several proposed refusals have already been built, measured against a real corpus, and rejected on precision\\\". scope.rs records exactly such a rejection for symbol-mention detection: flagging a module::symbol reference whose module never appears in the fact's captured output \\\"would have flagged a legitimate quotation ... while missing both real overreaches\\\"; \\\"Naming a symbol the code you read calls is normal; concluding about what that symbol does is the failure — and those two are not distinguishable by string matching.\\\" scope.rs also states the report-vs-refusal discriminator in this project's own words: the note-outside-extent check is \\\"human-owed, never a refusal\\\" because \\\"a note may legitimately name a location as context ... and only a person can tell that from an unsupported inference. Refusing would make the honest case unwritable to catch the dishonest one.\\\"\",\"extent\":[{\"key\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/report.rs\",\"label\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/report.rs lines 1-130\",\"world_root\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel\",\"world_state\":\"git:7c8965b105b644cbdac2f4f631373fd31ab2e7f8805fa51cb26d60e9746c7db2\"},{\"key\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/checks.rs\",\"label\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/checks.rs lines 1-200\",\"world_root\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel\",\"world_state\":\"git:7c8965b105b644cbdac2f4f631373fd31ab2e7f8805fa51cb26d60e9746c7db2\"},{\"key\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/README.md\",\"label\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/README.md lines 100-160\",\"world_root\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel\",\"world_state\":\"git:7c8965b105b644cbdac2f4f631373fd31ab2e7f8805fa51cb26d60e9746c7db2\"},{\"key\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/scope.rs\",\"label\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/scope.rs lines 38-73\",\"world_root\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel\",\"world_state\":\"git:7c8965b105b644cbdac2f4f631373fd31ab2e7f8805fa51cb26d60e9746c7db2\"}],\"output\":\"//! Renders the output contract: exactly two labelled partitions, each\\n//! stating its own scope on the same line, no standalone document-level\\n//! verdict anywhere. See the crate README / design memo for the contract\\n//! this implements.\\n\\nuse crate::checks::Findings;\\nuse crate::parse::Document;\\nuse crate::snapshot::Provenance;\\n\\n/// Exit codes. 0 and 1 are the conventional pass/fail; 2 is the D8 state —\\n/// \\\"no tetel rows found\\\" — which must never be confusable with clean.\\npub const EXIT_CLEAN: i32 = 0;\\npub const EXIT_CHECK_FAILED: i32 = 1;\\npub const EXIT_NO_ROWS: i32 = 2;\\n\\nconst NON_COVERAGE: &[&str] = &[\\n    \\\"dependents that never declared themselves\\\",\\n    \\\"deleted premises\\\",\\n    \\\"one word used in two senses\\\",\\n    \\\"a command that runs green without establishing its proposition\\\",\\n    \\\"unfalsifiable-shaped claim phrasing\\\",\\n];\\n\\n/// `build` names the binary that produced this report (see `buildid.rs`).\\n/// It is passed in rather than read here so this function stays a pure\\n/// function of the document it grades — and it is *always* printed,\\n/// including on the no-rows path, because the whole point is that two\\n/// outputs which disagree can be told apart by their checker. A report\\n/// that does not name its build cannot be disbelieved.\\npub fn render(display_path: &str, doc: &Document, findings: &Findings, build: &str) -> (i32, String) {\\n    if doc.row_groups_found == 0 && findings.ledger_claims_found == 0 {\\n        let msg = format!(\\n            \\\"no tetel rows found in {display_path} — out of scope, nothing was checked. \\\\\\nThis is a distinct state from a clean run, not a weaker way of spelling it (exit {EXIT_NO_ROWS}).\\\\n\\\\\\n\\\\nchecked by {build}\\\\n\\\"\\n        );\\n        return (EXIT_NO_ROWS, msg);\\n    }\\n\\n    let mut out = String::new();\\n\\n    // --- machine-checked partition -----------------------------------\\n    let failing = findings.machine_check_failed();\\n    let total_failures = findings.grammar_errors.len()\\n        + findings.subset_failures.len()\\n        + findings.abutting_failures.len()\\n        + findings.unsettled_failures.len()\\n        + findings.cascade_failures.len()\\n        + findings.ledger_errors.len()\\n        + findings.verdict_disagreements.len()\\n        + findings.out_of_proof.len()\\n        + usize::from(findings.provenance_failed());\\n    let scope = \\\"grammar, subset (enumerated rows only), abutting literals, unsettled citations, \\\\\\ndependency cascades, evidence-ledger import, verdict disagreement, claims out of proof, provenance drift\\\";\\n    if failing {\\n        out.push_str(&format!(\\n            \\\"machine-checked: {total_failures} failing — {scope}\\\\n\\\"\\n        ));\\n        for e in &findings.grammar_errors {\\n            out.push_str(&format!(\\\"  - [grammar] {e}\\\\n\\\"));\\n        }\\n        for e in &findings.subset_failures {\\n            out.push_str(&format!(\\\"  - [subset] {e}\\\\n\\\"));\\n        }\\n        for e in &findings.abutting_failures {\\n            out.push_str(&format!(\\\"  - [abutting-literal] {e}\\\\n\\\"));\\n        }\\n        for e in &findings.unsettled_failures {\\n            out.push_str(&format!(\\\"  - [unsettled-citation] {e}\\\\n\\\"));\\n        }\\n        for e in &findings.cascade_failures {\\n            out.push_str(&format!(\\\"  - [cascade] {e}\\\\n\\\"));\\n        }\\n        for e in &findings.ledger_errors {\\n            out.push_str(&format!(\\\"  - [ledger] {e}\\\\n\\\"));\\n        }\\n        for e in &findings.verdict_disagreements {\\n            out.push_str(&format!(\\\"  - [verdict-disagreement] {e}\\\\n\\\"));\\n        }\\n        for e in &findings.out_of_proof {\\n            out.push_str(&format!(\\\"  - [out-of-proof] {e}\\\\n\\\"));\\n        }\\n        match &findings.provenance {\\n            Provenance::Drifted { first_diff_line, snapshot_lines, memo_lines } => {\\n                let where_ = match first_diff_line {\\n                    Some(n) => format!(\\\"first difference at line {n}\\\"),\\n                    None => \\\"identical line-for-line but different lengths\\\".to_string(),\\n                };\\n                out.push_str(&format!(\\n                    \\\"  - [provenance-drift] this document is not what its own snapshot renders \\\\\\n({where_}; snapshot {snapshot_lines} lines, document {memo_lines}). Either the document was \\\\\\nedited by hand after rendering, or the workspace moved on without a re-render — a reader \\\\\\nfollowing a citation would land somewhere this text was never produced from. Re-render, or \\\\\\nrecover the workspace the text really came from.\\\\n\\\"\\n                ));\\n            }\\n            Provenance::Unreadable(e) => {\\n                out.push_str(&format!(\\n                    \\\"  - [provenance-drift] a snapshot exists beside this document but could not \\\\\\nbe rendered from ({e}) — reported rather than passed over, because an unreadable record is not \\\\\\na matching one.\\\\n\\\"\\n                ));\\n            }\\n            Provenance::Missing | Provenance::Matches => {}\\n        }\\n    } else {\\n        out.push_str(&format!(\\\"machine-checked: clean — {scope}\\\\n\\\"));\\n    }\\n    if !findings.abutting_candidates.is_empty() {\\n        out.push_str(\\\"  informational, not checked (never a failure, at any distance looser than abutting):\\\\n\\\");\\n        for c in &findings.abutting_candidates {\\n            out.push_str(&format!(\\\"    - {c}\\\\n\\\"));\\n        }\\n    }\\n\\n    out.push('\\\\n');\\n\\n    // --- human-owed partition -----------------------------------------\\n    out.push_str(\\n        \\\"human-owed: every READING/OBSERVED/ATTESTED row, every row whose domain or extent \\\\\\ncontains a proc:/external designator, the working-tree states this memo's facts were taken \\\\\\nagainst, the RUN command\\\\u{2194}proposition correspondence, \\\\\\ncited-but-undefined and defined-but-uncited ids, ungrounded ledger claims, claims grounded only \\\\\\nby attested (ingested) evidence, evidence sources that do not resolve, ledger claims with no \\\\\\ndeclared scope at all, qualified verdicts, superseded evidence, facts whose note names a location outside their own \\\\\\n captured extent, refusals recorded in a fact's own mint window, \\\\\\nand tetel's own standing non-coverage \\\\u{2014} none of this is settled by a passing check\\\\n\\\",\\n    );\\n    for e in &findings.superseded_evidence {\\n        out.push_str(&format!(\\\"  - superseded evidence: {e}\\\\n\\\"));\\n//! Runs the five reddening checks plus the report-only observations\\n//! (cited-but-undefined, defined-but-uncited) against a parsed [`Document`].\\n//!\\n//! Check 5 (the dependency-propagation cascade) shares its citation\\n//! syntax with check 4 but not its scope: check 4 owns direct, hop-1\\n//! prose citations of an unsettled row. Check 5 owns everything check 4\\n//! cannot see — row→row citation edges (found inside a row's own\\n//! `claim`/`note` fields, at any hop, including hop 1) and any citation,\\n//! prose or row, reached transitively at hop ≥ 2. Between them every\\n//! citation of an unsettled row is caught exactly once.\\n\\nuse std::collections::{HashMap, HashSet, VecDeque};\\nuse std::path::Path;\\n\\nuse crate::citations::{\\n    abutting_context, citation_ids_in, normalize_literal, scan_citations, AbuttingContext, Citation,\\n};\\nuse crate::evidence::{EvidenceRecord, Source, Verdict};\\nuse crate::ledger::Claim;\\nuse crate::model::{Designator, Kind, Row, Status};\\nuse crate::parse::Document;\\n\\n/// Everything the report needs, already computed. Kept as plain owned\\n/// data (not references into `Document`) so it's simple to assert on in\\n/// tests and simple to render.\\npub struct Findings {\\n    /// Formatted `line N: message`, sorted by line — check 1.\\n    pub grammar_errors: Vec<String>,\\n    /// Formatted failures — check 2, enumerated rows only.\\n    pub subset_failures: Vec<String>,\\n    /// Rows check 2 could not run on at all: `(id, claim)`.\\n    pub coverage_skipped: Vec<(String, String)>,\\n    /// Formatted failures — check 3.\\n    pub abutting_failures: Vec<String>,\\n    /// Formatted informational near-misses for check 3 (never fail).\\n    pub abutting_candidates: Vec<String>,\\n    /// Formatted failures — check 4.\\n    pub unsettled_failures: Vec<String>,\\n    /// Formatted failures — check 5. One entry per unsettled root that\\n    /// has at least one qualifying dependent, each entry a multi-line\\n    /// block naming every dependent under that root (grouped, not one\\n    /// finding per dependent — see the module doc comment).\\n    pub cascade_failures: Vec<String>,\\n    /// IDs cited in prose with no matching row.\\n    pub cited_undefined: Vec<String>,\\n    /// Rows never cited anywhere: `(id, claim)`.\\n    pub defined_uncited: Vec<(String, String)>,\\n    /// READING/OBSERVED/ATTESTED rows: `(id, \\\"KIND/STATUS\\\", claim)`.\\n    pub human_owed_rows: Vec<(String, String, String)>,\\n    /// IDs of every RUN row, for the class-level correspondence line.\\n    pub run_row_ids: Vec<String>,\\n\\n    // --- the grounding brief/record/check slice ---------------------\\n    /// How many claims the memo's evidence ledger table(s) carried, valid\\n    /// or not — distinguishes \\\"no ledger in this file\\\" from \\\"a ledger\\n    /// with zero rows\\\" for the report's early-exit guard.\\n    pub ledger_claims_found: usize,\\n    /// Formatted `line N: message` — ledger rows that could not be\\n    /// parsed at all (wrong cell count, empty id, duplicate id). Never\\n    /// silently dropped.\\n    pub ledger_errors: Vec<String>,\\n    /// A ledger claim with no evidence record at all: `(id, proposition)`.\\n    /// Human-owed — absence of evidence is not itself a failure.\\n    pub ungrounded_claims: Vec<(String, String)>,\\n    /// A ledger claim grounded, but only by evidence that derives to\\n    /// [`Kind::Attested`] for standing — today, that's *every* grounded\\n    /// claim, since ingestion (`tetel record`) is the only write path and\\n    /// every ingested record derives to `Attested` regardless of what it\\n    /// claims (see `evidence::EvidenceRecord::derived_kind`). Distinct\\n    /// from `ungrounded_claims`: this is \\\"someone looked, off-instrument\\\",\\n    /// not \\\"nobody looked\\\". `(id, proposition)`, human-owed, never a\\n    /// failure — it stops being the whole list only once a witnessed\\n    /// grounding exists to sit beside ingested ones.\\n    pub attested_grounded_claims: Vec<(String, String)>,\\n    /// One line per evidence record whose `source` is a path that does not\\n    /// resolve on disk. Human-owed, never a failure — mirrors the\\n    /// non-failing disposition of `cited_undefined`, one line per record\\n    /// (not aggregated into a bracketed list) since each names a distinct\\n    /// claim and path.\\n    pub unresolved_evidence_sources: Vec<String>,\\n    /// A ledger claim whose Domain/Extent cells are exactly\\n    /// [`crate::ledger::NO_SCOPE_DECLARED`] — minted by `tetel claim` via\\n    /// `compose::render`, which has no scope field to draw from.\\n    /// Human-owed, never a failure: distinct from `coverage_skipped`\\n    /// (a *declared* domain/extent this crate's checks can't enumerate,\\n    /// e.g. a `proc:`/`external:` designator) — this is a domain/extent\\n    /// that was never declared at all, and it says so plainly rather than\\n    /// let a silently-passing check imply a coverage claim nobody made.\\n    pub no_scope_claims: Vec<(String, String)>,\\n    /// Formatted failures: two evidence records for one claim disagreeing\\n    /// on verdict, or a verdict contradicting the author's own Status\\n    /// cell. A machine failure — an unresolved contradiction, the same\\n    /// shape as the unsettled-citation check.\\n    pub verdict_disagreements: Vec<String>,\\n    /// Whether the memo still matches the workspace snapshot beside it.\\n    /// See [`crate::snapshot`] for why a rendered document is not\\n    /// self-contained and what a mismatch means.\\n    pub provenance: crate::snapshot::Provenance,\\n    /// Whether the memo cites anything at all. A document with no\\n    /// citations owes no snapshot, so a missing one is only worth\\n    /// reporting when something in the text points into a workspace.\\n    pub cites_something: bool,\\n    /// Facts whose note names a location their own captured extent does\\n    /// not cover. Human-owed: see [`crate::scope`] for why this is a\\n    /// scope check rather than a truth check, and why it never refuses.\\n    /// Only populated when a snapshot shipped beside the memo, since\\n    /// facts appear nowhere in the rendered document.\\n    pub notes_outside_extent: Vec<crate::scope::OutsideExtent>,\\n    /// Refusals recorded between one fact's mint and the previous one —\\n    /// what the author tried and could not do in the window that produced\\n    /// each fact. Human-owed and verbatim; a mint following a refusal is\\n    /// frequently correct. Like `notes_outside_extent`, only populated\\n    /// when a snapshot shipped beside the memo, and only as complete as\\n    /// the log inside it.\\n    pub mint_windows: Vec<crate::facts::MintWindow>,\\n    /// Working trees this memo's facts observed in more than one state,\\n    /// and the facts that saw each. A record rather than a warning — two\\n    /// facts seeing one repository in two states is the normal condition\\n    /// of any design that outlives an edit. What it settles is whether\\n    /// two facts asserting opposite things about the same code disagree\\n    /// about the world or about the same world, which before this the\\n    /// ledger could not answer at all. Same snapshot dependency as\\n    /// `mint_windows`.\\n    pub tree_states: Vec<crate::worldstate::RootStates>,\\n    /// Facts whose markers predate roots being recorded, so their tree\\n    /// state describes the process's working directory rather than what\\n    /// they read. Reported rather than passed over: an ungradable record\\n    /// is not a matching one.\\n    pub tree_ungradable: Vec<String>,\\n    /// True when the memo's ledger has no scope columns for any claim to\\n    /// declare into — a tetel-authored ledger. Reported once at document\\n    /// level rather than once per claim; see\\n    /// [`claims_without_declared_scope`] for why that changed.\\n    pub ledger_has_no_scope_columns: bool,\\n    /// One line per grounded claim describing how its evidence stands:\\n    /// witnessed (extent captured by this tool, in a named workspace) or\\n    /// ingested (extent typed by a caller). Human-owed, printed item by\\n    /// item, never a failure — see [`grounding_provenance`].\\n    pub grounding_provenance: Vec<String>,\\n    /// `(claim id, pass, note)` per record whose verdict is\\n    /// [`Verdict::Qualifies`]. Human-owed, never a failure.\\n    ///\\n    /// Until this existed, a qualifying verdict was invisible. On the\\n    /// first real independent grounding pass, the two claims the grounder\\n    /// declined to confirm — because the numbers they rest on could not\\n    /// be verified from what it was given — printed as plainly \\\"grounded\\\",\\n    /// while the one claim where it qualified a single premise of a\\n    /// multi-premise argument was the only thing reddening the report.\\n    /// The check fired on the wrong claim and was silent on the right\\n    /// ones.\\n    pub qualified_claims: Vec<(String, String, String)>,\\n    /// Claims **out of proof**: every record on file grades a wording this\\n    /// claim no longer carries, and nothing has graded the current one.\\n    /// The proof-house sense exactly — the stamp no longer certifies this\\n    /// barrel. A machine failure; see [`analyze_ledger`].\\n    ///\\n    /// The remedy is to **reprove**: ground the claim again against what it\\n    /// now says. Nothing else clears it, and nothing clears it silently —\\n    /// the ledger is append-only, so the superseding record is added rather\\n    /// than the stale one edited.\\n    pub out_of_proof: Vec<String>,\\n    /// Records that graded an earlier wording of a claim which *has* since\\n    /// been reproved. Human-owed history, never a failure — the claim is\\n    /// back in proof, and these are the marks from before it was. See\\n    /// [`analyze_ledger`] on why the distinction matters.\\n    pub superseded_evidence: Vec<String>,\\n}\\n\\nimpl Findings {\\n    pub fn machine_check_failed(&self) -> bool {\\n        !self.grammar_errors.is_empty()\\n            || !self.subset_failures.is_empty()\\n            || !self.abutting_failures.is_empty()\\n            || !self.unsettled_failures.is_empty()\\n            || !self.cascade_failures.is_empty()\\n            || !self.ledger_errors.is_empty()\\n            || !self.verdict_disagreements.is_empty()\\n            || !self.out_of_proof.is_empty()\\n            || self.provenance_failed()\\n    }\\n\\n    /// Drift and an unreadable snapshot are machine failures: both are\\n    /// objective contradictions between a document and the record it\\n    /// claims to rest on, decidable without a human.\\n    ///\\n    /// A *missing* snapshot is deliberately not a failure. Every memo\\n    /// authored before `render --out` existed lacks one, and turning that\\n    /// into a hard failure would grade the tooling's own history rather\\n    /// than the document. It is reported as human-owed instead.\\n    pub fn provenance_failed(&self) -> bool {\\n        matches!(\\n            self.provenance,\\n            crate::snapshot::Provenance::Drifted { .. }\\n                | crate::snapshot::Provenance::Unreadable(_)\\n        )\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nfn covers(extent: &[Designator], d: &Designator) -> bool {\\n    extent.iter().any(|e| match (e, d) {\\n| **claim** | a proposition resting on one or more facts |\\n| **grounding** | an independent pass grading claims from source alone, with the author's scope withheld |\\n| **out of proof** | a claim whose every record grades a wording it no longer carries. The stamp no longer certifies this barrel — a machine failure |\\n| **reprove** | ground a claim again against what it now says. The only thing that clears *out of proof*, and it adds a record rather than editing one |\\n| **superseded** | the marks from before a claim was reproved. History, human-owed, never a failure |\\n| **witnessed / ingested** | whether the tool captured the act itself, or only captured someone *reporting* the act |\\n\\nThose three describe one state machine: revise a claim and it falls **out of proof**; **reprove** it\\nand the earlier records become **superseded**. The ledger is append-only, so nothing is ever cleared\\nby editing — only by adding a later proof.\\n\\n## What `check` tells you\\n\\nTwo labelled partitions, each stating its own scope, and **never a single document-level verdict**:\\n\\n- **machine-checked** — grammar, scope subset on enumerated rows, abutting literals, unsettled\\n  citations, dependency cascades, evidence-ledger import, verdict disagreement, claims out of proof,\\n  and provenance drift between a document and its own snapshot. These fail the run.\\n- **human-owed** — ungrounded claims, qualified verdicts in the grounder's own words, whether a claim\\n  was graded by the workspace that authored it or an independent one, notes reaching past their\\n  fact's extent, refusals recorded in a fact's mint window, and tetel's own standing non-coverage.\\n  **None of it is settled by a passing check**, and none of it fails the run.\\n\\nExit 2 means no tetel rows were found at all — out of scope, nothing checked, which is *not* a clean\\nrun.\\n\\n## Constraints — the things this must not become\\n\\n- **No auto-bless.** A stored value that silently updates to match a fresh run turns *\\\"the claim still\\n  holds\\\"* into *\\\"the command exited zero\\\"*. Those are different propositions, and the second certifies\\n  drift with a green checkmark. A mismatch is always a loud failure a human resolves.\\n- **No green wall.** Mechanical green on the checkable claims leaks confidence onto a document whose\\n  worst defects are the uncheckable ones. Output partitions, and prints the human-owed list item by\\n  item. **The job is to concentrate human reading on the residue, not to shrink it.**\\n- **Never paraphrase.** If a tool returns a clause, it returns the clause's prose. The moment the\\n  server summarises, every downstream reader inherits the tool's reading of the document instead of\\n  the document — silently.\\n- **Prose is never generated from fields.** Facts may be captured and interpolated; arguments may not\\n  be assembled out of structure. A format that constrains what a design can express constrains what\\n  can be argued about it, and the strongest refutations on record came from readers engaging prose\\n  with full generality. Whoever writes it — person or agent — writes the argument themselves.\\n- **Refusals are format-level, never heuristic.** \\\"You did not point at your evidence\\\" is a decidable\\n  question. \\\"This looks wrong\\\" is not, and belongs in the human-owed partition or nowhere.\\n\\n## Direction\\n\\nRoughly in order, and each gated on measurement rather than enthusiasm — several proposed refusals\\nhave already been built, measured against a real corpus, and **rejected on precision**:\\n\\n- **Make the guards authoritative.** A commit hook runs the pair-guards locally today; CI would make\\n  them hold for a contributor who is not using the same setup.\\n- **More authoring-time refusals, where they are decidable.** Requiring a workspace-rooted search\\n  behind a claim that names a symbol; requiring a transplanted mechanism to carry its donor's stated\\n  premises; reporting prose revised since its claims were last grounded.\\n- **Supporting-span selection.** At mint time, point at the span of captured output that supports the\\n  assertion — refused unless it is a verbatim substring of a cited fact. The dual of the extent\\n  guarantee: the author cannot type an extent, and here could not invent support, only select it.\\n- **An obligation ledger.** Today a warning is a printed string with no lifecycle, so a warning that\\n  was ignored and one that was correctly resolved are indistinguishable in every artifact. Warnings\\n  raised, discharged with a *type*, and what is still owed reported by `check`.\\n- **Then, and only then, an optional model advisory pass** — a smaller model warning that a claim\\n//! # What this is not\\n//!\\n//! It is a **scope** check, never a truth check. Whether the note is\\n//! *correct* about the location it names is exactly the limit this\\n//! project registered as permanently out of reach (\\\"insincere\\n//! examination\\\" — capture makes the extent honest, not the\\n//! interpretation). Nothing here touches that.\\n//!\\n//! It is also **human-owed, never a refusal**. A note may legitimately\\n//! name a location as context, contrast, or a pointer to what to read\\n//! next, and only a person can tell that from an unsupported inference.\\n//! Refusing would make the honest case unwritable to catch the dishonest\\n//! one.\\n//!\\n//! # What it catches, measured, and what it does not\\n//!\\n//! Of the two overreaching facts in the memo that motivated it, this\\n//! catches **one**. F6 named a file and a line range (`graph_utils.rs\\n//! ~560-566`) it never opened, which is the strong tell and fires here.\\n//! F7's note asserted the client \\\"mirrors the server's\\\" walk while its\\n//! extent covered only the client — an overreach that names no location\\n//! at all, and so is invisible to any check of this shape.\\n//!\\n//! One refinement was tried and rejected on the evidence: flagging a\\n//! `module::symbol` reference whose module never appears in the fact's\\n//! own captured output. Measured against the same three facts, it would\\n//! have flagged a legitimate quotation (F3 citing `object_utils::\\n//! load_parcel`, a call site inside the code it did read) while missing\\n//! both real overreaches, because `graph_utils` and `merge_utils` do\\n//! appear in the read source as call sites. Naming a symbol the code you\\n//! read calls is normal; concluding about what that symbol *does* is the\\n//! failure — and those two are not distinguishable by string matching.\\n//!\\n//! So: a file-and-line reference to unopened code is caught, and a\\n//! conclusion drawn without naming anything is not. That is a real\\n//! ceiling, not a to-do.\",\"pin\":\"sha256:9c2b665dede6e49b797a3c33e8945ebe29982e274b26ae9b610739465024c25b\",\"timestamp\":1786131150}\n8:{\"event\":\"Create\",\"id\":\"F7\",\"note\":\"Confirmed against the record types themselves: tetel has no notion of a recommendation, and no kind or class on either record a memo is made of. claims.rs: a Claim is { id, prop, from (cited fact ids), withdrawn, revisions } and ClaimEvent::Create carries only id/prop/from/timestamp — a proposition resting on facts, nothing typing what kind of proposition it is. prose.rs: a Block is { id, heading, level, text, cite, revisions } — prose \\\"is where the author talks, not where they're audited\\\", creation is deliberately ungated, and the only structure a block carries is heading-ness, depth, text and claim citations. Nothing on either surface marks a claim or a paragraph as a recommendation, an instruction, or anything else. Also relevant precedents captured here: claim refuses without --cites and on unknown fact ids (so citing-a-real-record refusals are the established authoring shape), and prose.rs documents the --before insertion design and the append-only revise-with-why discipline.\",\"extent\":[{\"key\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/claims.rs\",\"label\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/claims.rs\",\"world_root\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel\",\"world_state\":\"git:7c8965b105b644cbdac2f4f631373fd31ab2e7f8805fa51cb26d60e9746c7db2\"},{\"key\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/prose.rs\",\"label\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/prose.rs lines 1-120\",\"world_root\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel\",\"world_state\":\"git:7c8965b105b644cbdac2f4f631373fd31ab2e7f8805fa51cb26d60e9746c7db2\"}],\"output\":\"//! `tetel claim` — assert, revise, or withdraw a proposition resting on\\n//! facts. Refuses without `--cites` (a claim must cite at least one\\n//! fact) and refuses on an unknown fact id. Before accepting a new\\n//! claim, prints an overlap report: existing facts, not among those\\n//! cited, that share an overlap key (see `pending.rs`'s doc comment on\\n//! fix 2) with the ones being cited — this is how the tool surfaces\\n//! evidence the author may have forgotten they already had.\\n//!\\n//! `--revise`/`--withdraw` are append-only, mirroring `tclaim`: both\\n//! refuse without `--why`, and a revision keeps the old proposition in\\n//! the log rather than overwriting it.\\n\\nuse std::collections::BTreeSet;\\nuse std::io;\\nuse std::path::{Path, PathBuf};\\n\\nuse serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};\\n\\nuse crate::facts;\\nuse crate::workspace::{self, AuthoringError, Kind};\\n\\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\\n#[serde(tag = \\\"event\\\")]\\npub enum ClaimEvent {\\n    Create {\\n        id: String,\\n        prop: String,\\n        from: Vec<String>,\\n        timestamp: u64,\\n    },\\n    Revise {\\n        id: String,\\n        #[serde(default)]\\n        prop: Option<String>,\\n        #[serde(default)]\\n        from: Option<Vec<String>>,\\n        why: String,\\n        timestamp: u64,\\n    },\\n    Withdraw {\\n        id: String,\\n        why: String,\\n        timestamp: u64,\\n    },\\n}\\n\\npub struct Claim {\\n    pub id: String,\\n    pub prop: String,\\n    pub from: Vec<String>,\\n    pub withdrawn: bool,\\n    pub revisions: usize,\\n}\\n\\nfn log_path(workspace_dir: &Path) -> PathBuf {\\n    workspace_dir.join(\\\"claims.jsonl\\\")\\n}\\n\\npub fn load_all(workspace_dir: &Path) -> io::Result<Vec<Claim>> {\\n    let events: Vec<ClaimEvent> = workspace::read_jsonl(&log_path(workspace_dir))?;\\n    let mut by_id: std::collections::BTreeMap<String, Claim> = std::collections::BTreeMap::new();\\n    let mut order: Vec<String> = Vec::new();\\n    for ev in events {\\n        match ev {\\n            ClaimEvent::Create { id, prop, from, .. } => {\\n                order.push(id.clone());\\n                by_id.insert(id.clone(), Claim { id, prop, from, withdrawn: false, revisions: 0 });\\n            }\\n            ClaimEvent::Revise { id, prop, from, .. } => {\\n                if let Some(c) = by_id.get_mut(&id) {\\n                    if let Some(p) = prop {\\n                        c.prop = p;\\n                    }\\n                    if let Some(f) = from {\\n                        c.from = f;\\n                    }\\n                    c.revisions += 1;\\n                }\\n            }\\n            ClaimEvent::Withdraw { id, .. } => {\\n                if let Some(c) = by_id.get_mut(&id) {\\n                    c.withdrawn = true;\\n                }\\n            }\\n        }\\n    }\\n    Ok(order.into_iter().filter_map(|id| by_id.remove(&id)).collect())\\n}\\n\\npub fn exists(workspace_dir: &Path, id: &str) -> io::Result<bool> {\\n    Ok(load_all(workspace_dir)?.iter().any(|c| c.id == id))\\n}\\n\\nfn parse_ids(csv: &str) -> Vec<String> {\\n    csv.split(',').map(str::trim).filter(|s| !s.is_empty()).map(String::from).collect()\\n}\\n\\npub struct CreateOutcome {\\n    pub claim: Claim,\\n    /// `(fact id, current note)` for every non-cited fact sharing a\\n    /// designator with the cited ones.\\n    pub overlap: Vec<(String, String)>,\\n}\\n\\n/// `tetel claim --proposition <text> --cites F1,F3`.\\npub fn create(workspace_dir: &Path, prop: &str, from_csv: &str) -> Result<CreateOutcome, AuthoringError> {\\n    let ids = parse_ids(from_csv);\\n    if ids.is_empty() {\\n        return Err(workspace::refuse(\\n            workspace_dir,\\n            \\\"claim\\\",\\n            \\\"a claim must rest on at least one fact (--cites was missing or empty); try `tetel query facts` to find one, or mint a new fact with `tetel look`/`tetel run` + `tetel fact`\\\",\\n        ));\\n    }\\n    let mut missing = Vec::new();\\n    for id in &ids {\\n        if !facts::exists(workspace_dir, id)? {\\n            missing.push(id.clone());\\n        }\\n    }\\n    if !missing.is_empty() {\\n        return Err(workspace::refuse(\\n            workspace_dir,\\n            \\\"claim\\\",\\n            format!(\\\"cited fact(s) do not exist: {}; try `tetel query facts` to see what exists\\\", missing.join(\\\", \\\")),\\n        ));\\n    }\\n    if prop.trim().is_empty() {\\n        return Err(workspace::refuse(workspace_dir, \\\"claim\\\", \\\"no --proposition given; a claim needs a proposition\\\"));\\n    }\\n\\n    let overlap = overlap_report(workspace_dir, &ids)?;\\n\\n    let id = workspace::next_id(workspace_dir, Kind::Claim)?;\\n    let event = ClaimEvent::Create { id: id.clone(), prop: prop.to_string(), from: ids.clone(), timestamp: workspace::now_unix() };\\n    workspace::append_jsonl(&log_path(workspace_dir), &event)?;\\n\\n    Ok(CreateOutcome { claim: Claim { id, prop: prop.to_string(), from: ids, withdrawn: false, revisions: 0 }, overlap })\\n}\\n\\n/// Every fact not among `cited_ids` that shares an extent key with the\\n/// union of `cited_ids`'s own extents (fix 2: the key is a resolved\\n/// path where a designator names a file, so three different line-ranges\\n/// of one file, and a plain read of it, all overlap each other now,\\n/// where the prototype's literal-command-string key never caught this).\\nfn overlap_report(workspace_dir: &Path, cited_ids: &[String]) -> io::Result<Vec<(String, String)>> {\\n    let all = facts::load_all(workspace_dir)?;\\n    let cited: BTreeSet<&str> = cited_ids.iter().map(String::as_str).collect();\\n    let mut union_keys: BTreeSet<&str> = BTreeSet::new();\\n    for f in &all {\\n        if cited.contains(f.id.as_str()) {\\n            for e in &f.extent {\\n                union_keys.insert(e.key.as_str());\\n            }\\n        }\\n    }\\n    let mut out = Vec::new();\\n    for f in &all {\\n        if cited.contains(f.id.as_str()) {\\n            continue;\\n        }\\n        if f.extent.iter().any(|e| union_keys.contains(e.key.as_str())) {\\n            out.push((f.id.clone(), f.note.clone()));\\n        }\\n    }\\n    Ok(out)\\n}\\n\\n/// `tetel claim --revise <id> --why <text> [--proposition <text>] [--cites ...]`.\\n/// At least one of `new_prop`/`new_from_csv` must be given — a revision\\n/// that changes nothing isn't a revision.\\npub fn revise(\\n    workspace_dir: &Path,\\n    id: &str,\\n    new_prop: Option<&str>,\\n    new_from_csv: Option<&str>,\\n    why: &str,\\n) -> Result<(), AuthoringError> {\\n    if !exists(workspace_dir, id)? {\\n        return Err(workspace::refuse(workspace_dir, \\\"claim\\\", format!(\\\"no such claim: {id}\\\")));\\n    }\\n    if why.trim().is_empty() {\\n        return Err(workspace::refuse(workspace_dir, \\\"claim\\\", \\\"--revise requires --why (revisions must explain themselves)\\\"));\\n    }\\n    let from = match new_from_csv {\\n        Some(csv) => {\\n            let ids = parse_ids(csv);\\n            let mut missing = Vec::new();\\n            for fid in &ids {\\n                if !facts::exists(workspace_dir, fid)? {\\n                    missing.push(fid.clone());\\n                }\\n            }\\n            if !missing.is_empty() {\\n                return Err(workspace::refuse(workspace_dir, \\\"claim\\\", format!(\\\"cited fact(s) do not exist: {}\\\", missing.join(\\\", \\\"))));\\n            }\\n            Some(ids)\\n        }\\n        None => None,\\n    };\\n    let prop = new_prop.map(str::to_string);\\n    if prop.is_none() && from.is_none() {\\n        return Err(workspace::refuse(workspace_dir, \\\"claim\\\", \\\"--revise requires a new --proposition and/or --cites\\\"));\\n    }\\n    let event = ClaimEvent::Revise { id: id.to_string(), prop, from, why: why.to_string(), timestamp: workspace::now_unix() };\\n    workspace::append_jsonl(&log_path(workspace_dir), &event)?;\\n    Ok(())\\n}\\n\\n/// `tetel claim --withdraw <id> --why <text>`.\\npub fn withdraw(workspace_dir: &Path, id: &str, why: &str) -> Result<(), AuthoringError> {\\n    if !exists(workspace_dir, id)? {\\n        return Err(workspace::refuse(workspace_dir, \\\"claim\\\", format!(\\\"no such claim: {id}\\\")));\\n    }\\n    if why.trim().is_empty() {\\n        return Err(workspace::refuse(workspace_dir, \\\"claim\\\", \\\"--withdraw requires --why\\\"));\\n    }\\n    let event = ClaimEvent::Withdraw { id: id.to_string(), why: why.to_string(), timestamp: workspace::now_unix() };\\n    workspace::append_jsonl(&log_path(workspace_dir), &event)?;\\n    Ok(())\\n}\\n\\n/// What `tetel claim` was asked to do — create, revise or withdraw. The\\n/// single shape both the CLI and the MCP server build and pass to\\n/// [`dispatch`], so the two front ends can never drift on which\\n/// combination of missing flags gets refused, or with what text.\\npub enum ClaimRequest {\\n    Create { prop: Option<String>, from: Option<String> },\\n    Revise { id: String, prop: Option<String>, from: Option<String>, why: Option<String> },\\n    Withdraw { id: String, why: Option<String> },\\n}\\n\\npub enum ClaimOutcome {\\n    Created(CreateOutcome),\\n    Revised { id: String },\\n    Withdrawn { id: String },\\n}\\n\\n/// Dispatches a [`ClaimRequest`] to [`create`], [`revise`] or\\n/// [`withdraw`], refusing with the exact text the CLI has always printed\\n/// for a flag a mode required but didn't get — lifted here (rather than\\n/// left as a bare `eprintln!` in `main.rs`) so the MCP server's\\n/// structured refusals carry the same guidance the CLI does, from the\\n/// one place that decides it.\\npub fn dispatch(workspace_dir: &Path, req: ClaimRequest) -> Result<ClaimOutcome, AuthoringError> {\\n    match req {\\n        ClaimRequest::Withdraw { id, why } => {\\n            let why = why.ok_or_else(|| workspace::refuse(workspace_dir, \\\"claim\\\", \\\"claim --withdraw requires --why\\\"))?;\\n            withdraw(workspace_dir, &id, &why).map(|()| ClaimOutcome::Withdrawn { id })\\n        }\\n        ClaimRequest::Revise { id, prop, from, why } => {\\n            let why = why.ok_or_else(|| workspace::refuse(workspace_dir, \\\"claim\\\", \\\"claim --revise requires --why\\\"))?;\\n            revise(workspace_dir, &id, prop.as_deref(), from.as_deref(), &why).map(|()| ClaimOutcome::Revised { id })\\n        }\\n        ClaimRequest::Create { prop, from } => {\\n            let prop = prop.ok_or_else(|| workspace::refuse(workspace_dir, \\\"claim\\\", \\\"claim requires --proposition\\\"))?;\\n            let from = from.ok_or_else(|| workspace::refuse(workspace_dir, \\\"claim\\\", \\\"claim requires --cites\\\"))?;\\n            create(workspace_dir, &prop, &from).map(ClaimOutcome::Created)\\n        }\\n    }\\n}\\n\\n//! `tetel prose` — compose the document's prose, one block at a time.\\n//! Creation is deliberately not gated (mirrors the prototype: prose is\\n//! where the author talks, not where they're audited — citing a\\n//! nonexistent claim isn't even checked here). Revision is gated the\\n//! same way `claims.rs`'s is: `--why` is required, and the superseded\\n//! text stays in the append-only log — `tetel render` only ever shows a\\n//! block's current text.\\n//!\\n//! # Document order, and why `--before` exists\\n//!\\n//! Insertion was dropped in this port on the grounds that none of the\\n//! four prototype runs ever used it — every block was composed and left\\n//! in append order. That reasoning had a selection effect in it, spotted\\n//! later by an agent writing with the tool: **those runs never needed\\n//! insertion because they wrote all their prose at the end**, which is\\n//! precisely the pattern the rhythm brief exists to prevent. The evidence\\n//! for removing the feature came from runs exhibiting the behaviour the\\n//! tool now tries to stop.\\n//!\\n//! Without insertion, document order is authoring order. An author\\n//! following the brief — writing a paragraph the moment a claim exists to\\n//! say something about — gets a document in discovery order, which reads\\n//! badly. The only way to get a well-ordered document is to defer prose\\n//! to the end. So the tool's shape rewarded the anti-pattern, and no\\n//! amount of brief text could outweigh that: a prompt asks, a shape\\n//! decides.\\n//!\\n//! `create` therefore takes an optional `before`, and document order is\\n//! the insertion order the log records rather than the order blocks were\\n//! appended. `tmove`-style reordering of existing blocks stays unported:\\n//! nothing has needed it, and unlike insertion its absence creates no\\n//! incentive to write badly.\\n\\nuse std::io;\\nuse std::path::{Path, PathBuf};\\n\\nuse serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};\\n\\nuse crate::workspace::{self, AuthoringError, Kind};\\n\\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\\n#[serde(tag = \\\"event\\\")]\\npub enum ProseEvent {\\n    Create {\\n        id: String,\\n        heading: bool,\\n        /// A markdown heading depth, 1..=6. `None` for a paragraph.\\n        /// Fix in this port: the prototype rendered every heading at one\\n        /// fixed depth (`##`), which two runs independently reported as\\n        /// a defect.\\n        #[serde(default)]\\n        level: Option<u8>,\\n        text: String,\\n        #[serde(default)]\\n        cite: Vec<String>,\\n        /// The block this one was inserted before, if any. Absent — the\\n        /// shape of every Create written before this field existed —\\n        /// means \\\"append\\\", so an old log replays exactly as it did.\\n        #[serde(default)]\\n        before: Option<String>,\\n        timestamp: u64,\\n    },\\n    Revise {\\n        id: String,\\n        text: String,\\n        why: String,\\n        /// Citations this revision sets, replacing whatever the block\\n        /// carried. Absent — the shape every revision written before this\\n        /// field existed has — leaves them untouched, so an old log\\n        /// replays exactly as it did.\\n        #[serde(default)]\\n        cite: Option<Vec<String>>,\\n        timestamp: u64,\\n    },\\n}\\n\\npub struct Block {\\n    pub id: String,\\n    pub heading: bool,\\n    pub level: Option<u8>,\\n    pub text: String,\\n    pub cite: Vec<String>,\\n    pub revisions: usize,\\n}\\n\\nfn log_path(workspace_dir: &Path) -> PathBuf {\\n    workspace_dir.join(\\\"prose.jsonl\\\")\\n}\\n\\n/// Every block, in creation order — which is also document order, since\\n/// this port has no reordering (see the module doc comment).\\npub fn load_all(workspace_dir: &Path) -> io::Result<Vec<Block>> {\\n    let events: Vec<ProseEvent> = workspace::read_jsonl(&log_path(workspace_dir))?;\\n    let mut by_id: std::collections::BTreeMap<String, Block> = std::collections::BTreeMap::new();\\n    let mut order: Vec<String> = Vec::new();\\n    for ev in events {\\n        match ev {\\n            ProseEvent::Create { id, heading, level, text, cite, before, .. } => {\\n                // Insert at the anchor if it is still present; otherwise\\n                // append. A dangling anchor cannot reorder silently — the\\n                // block lands at the end, where its absence is visible.\\n                match before.as_deref().and_then(|b| order.iter().position(|o| o == b)) {\\n                    Some(at) => order.insert(at, id.clone()),\\n                    None => order.push(id.clone()),\\n                }\\n                by_id.insert(id.clone(), Block { id, heading, level, text, cite, revisions: 0 });\\n            }\\n            ProseEvent::Revise { id, text, cite, .. } => {\\n                if let Some(b) = by_id.get_mut(&id) {\\n                    b.text = text;\\n                    // Absent means \\\"unchanged\\\", not \\\"cleared\\\": a\\n                    // revision written before this field existed must\\n                    // replay identically.\\n                    if let Some(c) = cite {\\n                        b.cite = c;\\n                    }\\n                    b.revisions += 1;\\n                }\\n            }\\n        }\",\"pin\":\"sha256:938695a2fc4d6b2030b4c22c71f476c58801ca7af554e675dfcf6f6706942e0f\",\"timestamp\":1786131599}\n9:{\"event\":\"Create\",\"id\":\"F8\",\"note\":\"Operational, cited by nothing: created the output directory docs/design/ in the tetel repo (mkdir -p, exit 0, no output) so render --out has somewhere to write this memo. Establishes nothing about the design; minted only so the act does not sit in the pending buffer being reported as an unminted observation on every render.\",\"extent\":[{\"key\":\"mkdir -p /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/docs/design\",\"label\":\"proc: mkdir -p /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/docs/design (exit 0)\",\"world_root\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel\",\"world_state\":\"git:7c8965b105b644cbdac2f4f631373fd31ab2e7f8805fa51cb26d60e9746c7db2\"}],\"output\":\"\",\"pin\":\"sha256:b1fc5f8047178c13ecf8b62f23da52d0bc7672d8550acfd18b67eb6a7c603b00\",\"timestamp\":1786131696}\n2:{\"event\":\"Create\",\"id\":\"P2\",\"heading\":false,\"level\":null,\"text\":\"All code observations in this memo were taken against the tetel repository at commit 57bd0e2 (\\\"ci: make the guards authoritative for the repository, not just for one editor\\\"), clean working tree. TET-28 asks for a refusal: a memo that recommends modifying a symbol must carry a repo-wide census of that symbol, captured by the tool, or the memo does not render. This memo settles two questions the ticket leaves open — what the trigger is and whether it is decidable, and what \\\"workspace-rooted\\\" means decidably — and reaches a design that keeps the refusal format-level.\",\"cite\":[\"C1\"],\"before\":null,\"timestamp\":1786130606}\n9:{\"event\":\"Create\",\"id\":\"P9\",\"heading\":false,\"level\":null,\"text\":\"What is decidable is a declaration. A modification target becomes a structured record — a symbol string plus a citation of the fact that censuses it — created by a new authoring verb and rendered as its own section, the way the evidence ledger and Facts table already render structured records without generating prose from fields. The refusal then never reads English at all: it fires when a declared target's cited fact carries no qualifying census entry, which is an objective contradiction between the document and the record it claims to rest on — the exact principle the machine partition already states for provenance drift, and the README's own example of the one decidable refusal shape, \\\"you did not point at your evidence\\\". The disposition follows the project's report-versus-refuse discriminator from the other side: the note-outside-extent check stayed human-owed because only a person can tell context from conclusion, but for a declared-and-uncensused target nothing is left to tell — the remedy is singular. Run the workspace-rooted grep for that symbol, mint the fact, cite it; or withdraw the target.\",\"cite\":[\"C7\"],\"before\":null,\"timestamp\":1786131246}\n10:{\"event\":\"Create\",\"id\":\"P10\",\"heading\":false,\"level\":null,\"text\":\"Declaration moves the vacuity question to under-declaration, and the honest answer is that no refusal can reach it: deciding that a memo recommended something it never declared is the same heuristic read of prose the trigger just rejected. The design's answer is visibility rather than detection, in two places. First, the section never disappears: once a workspace declares any target the section renders, and once any claim exists it renders even when empty, as \\\"Modification targets: none declared\\\" — so a memo full of tell-the-implementer language with an empty census section shows its hole to every reader, instead of an undeclared target being an invisible omission. Second, check's standing non-coverage list — whose first entry is already \\\"dependents that never declared themselves\\\" — gains the census's own blind spot, stated as such. The ticket also argues under-declaration is caught because the adversarial-review pass puts every tell-the-implementer claim in scope unconditionally; that is true of the review protocol this project runs around tetel, but it is a property of an agent definition outside this repository, so this design records it as the ticket's premise and deliberately does not lean on it: the rendered hole and the printed blind spot carry the visibility argument on their own.\",\"cite\":[\"C8\"],\"before\":null,\"timestamp\":1786131258}\n11:{\"event\":\"Create\",\"id\":\"P11\",\"heading\":false,\"level\":null,\"text\":\"The refusal lives in both surfaces that can see the workspace or its snapshot, because each covers the other's blind spot. render --out refuses — the render path's first content-conditional refusal, and deliberately so: its existing pending-observations warning stays a warning because only the author can tell a deliberate uncited look from a forgotten one, but an uncensused declared target has no honest reading to protect. That refusal must be implemented once, in a dispatch shared by CLI and MCP — this exact path has already drifted once, when workspace identity was minted by the CLI arm and not the MCP handler until the act moved into snapshot::write. check then re-verifies the rendered document against its snapshot and reports [uncensused-target] in the machine partition's scope line, which catches a hand-carried or stale document that never went through the refusing render. Like the other snapshot-dependent findings, the check can only run when a snapshot ships; without one, target rows are reported human-owed as unverifiable, consistent with the standing choice that a missing snapshot is human-owed rather than a machine failure.\",\"cite\":[\"C9\"],\"before\":null,\"timestamp\":1786131269}\n16:{\"event\":\"Create\",\"id\":\"P16\",\"heading\":false,\"level\":null,\"text\":\"check's report already carries a standing non-coverage list, printed item by item, and this feature adds its own blind spots to it rather than leaving them implicit. A textual census cannot see dynamic dispatch, re-exports or aliased imports, or callers assembled by macros and string concatenation. Its traversal is the system grep's — tetel shells out precisely to avoid owning a subtly different regex dialect — so the platform's rules for symlinked directories and the binary-file skip bound what a repo-wide search physically visited. Rooting establishes where the search started, never which tree it should have started in: whether the censused root is the tree the memo designs against remains a reader's question, answerable from the per-root tree-state report the check already prints. Whether anyone read the census is the insincere-examination ceiling. And under-declared targets are invisible by construction — the hole in the rendered section and the non-coverage line are their only trace. A reviewer who wants the census to mean more than \\\"a repo-wide list of textual match sites exists\\\" must read it, which is exactly the reading the Facts table was built to concentrate.\",\"cite\":[\"C12\"],\"before\":null,\"timestamp\":1786131443}\n18:{\"event\":\"Create\",\"id\":\"P18\",\"heading\":false,\"level\":null,\"text\":\"The ticket asks that this ship before any TET-27 co-evolution run, on the argument that findings the census would have surfaced must not be double-credited to a prototype, which would make that run's \\\"would existing machinery have caught this anyway\\\" kill-arm unevaluable. The premise of that argument lives in the tickets and the private review trail, not in this repository, so this memo does not grade it; what this memo can establish is that the sequencing costs nothing. The feature's surface is one recorded entry in the grep path, two serde-defaulted fields on the extent record, one new record type and verb, one rendered section, one shared refusal, and one machine check with its printed limits — and none of it touches or presupposes TET-27's subject matter: no code-to-graph links, no commit references, no dependency propagation. Shipping first is therefore safe whether or not the double-crediting argument is right, and the design was not bent to accommodate it. What this memo deliberately does not decide: any implementation detail beyond the record shapes and predicates above, and any widening of the trigger — in particular, no future measurement of a mention-based advisory should be allowed to harden into a refusal, because the measured evidence in this repository says that detector cannot tell quotation from conclusion.\",\"cite\":[\"C13\",\"C6\"],\"before\":null,\"timestamp\":1786131458}\n20:{\"event\":\"Revise\",\"id\":\"P2\",\"text\":\"All code observations in this memo were taken against the tetel repository at commit 57bd0e2 (\\\"ci: make the guards authoritative for the repository, not just for one editor\\\"), clean working tree. TET-28 asks for a refusal: a memo that tells an implementer to modify a symbol must carry a repo-wide census of that symbol, captured by the tool rather than typed, or the memo does not render. The motivating defect, from a private project's review trail and described here by its shape: a memo recommended modifying a function and spent a repo-wide negative about its callers, while the author's actual caller sweep — honest in its own fact — was scoped to a single file; one workspace-rooted grep would have landed immediately on a second production gate with a different mechanism, and the miss instead survived five review rounds before both attack passes found it independently. This memo settles the two questions the ticket leaves open — what the trigger is and whether it is decidable, and what \\\"workspace-rooted\\\" means decidably — and reaches a design that keeps the refusal format-level.\",\"why\":\"The introduction never said what defect motivates the rule, and the memo's public setting needs that stated by shape rather than by the private trail's names. One added sentence carries it; the rest is unchanged apart from tightening.\",\"cite\":null,\"timestamp\":1786131486}\n21:{\"event\":\"Revise\",\"id\":\"P11\",\"text\":\"The refusal lives at the earliest surface that can decide it: declaration. `tetel target` refuses to create a target whose cited fact has no qualifying census entry — the same authoring-time shape as `tetel fact` refusing on an empty pending buffer. A fact's extent is immutable once minted and a target immutable once declared, so this one refusal makes \\\"every declared target is censused\\\" a workspace invariant, and render needs no refusal of its own for workspace-authored documents: its job is to emit the section. What render cannot vouch for, check re-verifies — the rendered document against its own snapshot, reported as [uncensused-target] in the machine partition's scope line — which is what catches a hand-authored or tampered document that never passed through the refusing verb. Like the other snapshot-dependent findings, that check runs only when a snapshot ships; without one, target rows are reported human-owed as unverifiable, consistent with the standing choice that a missing snapshot is human-owed rather than a machine failure. The verb itself must be one dispatch shared by CLI and MCP, the pattern look and fact already use, because this render path has already recorded one CLI/MCP drift on a duplicated act.\",\"why\":\"C9 was revised: writing this section exposed that the render-path refusal guarded an unreachable state once the same predicate refuses at declaration time, where the house precedent (fact's empty-buffer refusal) already lives. The paragraph now carries the invariant argument instead of the render-refusal one.\",\"cite\":null,\"timestamp\":1786131535}\n24:{\"event\":\"Revise\",\"id\":\"P2\",\"text\":\"All code observations in this memo were taken against the tetel repository at commit 57bd0e2 (\\\"ci: make the guards authoritative for the repository, not just for one editor\\\"), clean working tree. TET-28 asks for a refusal: a memo that tells an implementer to modify a symbol must carry a repo-wide census of that symbol, captured by the tool rather than typed, or the memo does not render. The motivating defect, as the ticket reports it from a private project's review trail and described here only by its shape: a memo recommended modifying a function and spent a repo-wide negative about its callers, while the author's actual caller sweep — honest in its own fact — was scoped to a single file; per the ticket, one workspace-rooted grep would have landed on a second production gate with a different mechanism, and the miss instead survived five review rounds before both attack passes found it independently. This memo settles the two questions the ticket leaves open — what the trigger is and whether it is decidable, and what \\\"workspace-rooted\\\" means decidably — and reaches a design that keeps the refusal format-level.\",\"why\":\"The motivating-defect narrative was asserted in this memo's own voice while resting on no claim; attributing it explicitly to the ticket makes it a description of the assignment, which is what it is — this memo's evidence starts at the code.\",\"cite\":null,\"timestamp\":1786131639}\n25:{\"event\":\"Revise\",\"id\":\"P10\",\"text\":\"Declaration moves the vacuity question to under-declaration, and the honest answer is that no refusal can reach it: deciding that a memo recommended something it never declared is the same heuristic read of prose the trigger just rejected. The design's answer is visibility rather than detection, in two places. First, the section never disappears: once a workspace declares any target the section renders, and once any claim exists it renders even when empty, as \\\"Modification targets: none declared\\\" — so a memo full of tell-the-implementer language with an empty census section shows its hole to every reader, instead of an undeclared target being an invisible omission. Second, check's standing non-coverage list — whose first entry is already \\\"dependents that never declared themselves\\\" — gains the census's own blind spot, stated as such. The ticket offers a further reason to assess the risk as small: its review protocol's adversarial pass puts every tell-the-implementer claim in scope unconditionally. That is a property of an agent definition outside this repository, not of tetel, so this design records it as the ticket's premise and deliberately does not lean on it — the rendered hole and the printed blind spot carry the visibility argument on their own, for any consumer of the tool, with or without that protocol around it.\",\"why\":\"The paragraph endorsed the ticket's cone-rule premise as true (\\\"that is true of the review protocol...\\\") while no claim states it and its subject lives outside this repository; the design deliberately does not rest on it, so the endorsement goes.\",\"cite\":null,\"timestamp\":1786131650}\n26:{\"event\":\"Revise\",\"id\":\"P16\",\"text\":\"check's report already carries a standing non-coverage list, printed item by item, and this feature adds its own blind spots to it rather than leaving them implicit. A textual census cannot see dynamic dispatch, re-exports or aliased imports, or callers assembled by macros and string concatenation. Its traversal is the system grep's — tetel shells out precisely to avoid owning a subtly different regex dialect — so the platform's rules for symlinked directories and the binary-file skip bound what a repo-wide search physically visited. Rooting establishes where the search started, never which tree it should have started in: whether the censused root is the tree the memo designs against remains a reader's question, answerable from the per-root tree-state report the check already prints. Whether anyone read the census is the insincere-examination ceiling: a census is a list of sites, not an understanding of them. And under-declared targets are invisible by construction — the hole in the rendered section and the non-coverage line are their only trace.\",\"why\":\"The closing sentence asserted the Facts table's design purpose, which no cited claim states; the ticket's own \\\"a census is a list of sites, not an understanding of them\\\" is the honest close and stays within C12.\",\"cite\":null,\"timestamp\":1786131658}\n27:{\"event\":\"Revise\",\"id\":\"P2\",\"text\":\"All code observations in this memo were taken against the tetel repository at commit 57bd0e2 (\\\"ci: make the guards authoritative for the repository, not just for one editor\\\"); at the start of authoring the working tree was clean, and the memo you are reading plus its snapshot are the one addition this workspace makes to it. TET-28 asks for a refusal: a memo that tells an implementer to modify a symbol must carry a repo-wide census of that symbol, captured by the tool rather than typed, or the memo does not render. The motivating defect, as the ticket reports it from a private project's review trail and described here only by its shape: a memo recommended modifying a function and spent a repo-wide negative about its callers, while the author's actual caller sweep — honest in its own fact — was scoped to a single file; per the ticket, one workspace-rooted grep would have landed on a second production gate with a different mechanism, and the miss instead survived five review rounds before both attack passes found it independently. This memo settles the two questions the ticket leaves open — what the trigger is and whether it is decidable, and what \\\"workspace-rooted\\\" means decidably — and reaches a design that keeps the refusal format-level.\",\"why\":\"C1 was narrowed after grounding pass k1 qualified its \\\"clean working tree\\\" clause; the opening sentence now carries the same timing the claim does.\",\"cite\":null,\"timestamp\":1786132471}\n28:{\"event\":\"Revise\",\"id\":\"P18\",\"text\":\"The ticket asks that this ship before any TET-27 co-evolution run, on the argument that findings the census would have surfaced must not be double-credited to a prototype, which would make that run's \\\"would existing machinery have caught this anyway\\\" kill-arm unevaluable. The premise of that argument lives in the tickets and the private review trail, not in this repository, so this memo does not grade it; what this memo can establish is that the sequencing costs nothing. The feature's surface is one recorded entry in the grep path, two serde-defaulted fields on the extent record, one new record type and verb, one rendered section, one shared refusal, and one machine check with its printed limits — each an extension of a mechanism cited in this memo's facts, and none of them a code-to-graph link, a commit reference, or dependency propagation, the mechanisms the TET-27 ticket describes as its subject. Shipping first is therefore safe whether or not the double-crediting argument is right, and the design was not bent to accommodate it. What this memo deliberately does not decide: any implementation detail beyond the record shapes and predicates above, and any widening of the trigger — in particular, no future measurement of a mention-based advisory should be allowed to harden into a refusal, because the measured evidence in this repository says that detector cannot tell quotation from conclusion.\",\"why\":\"C13 was narrowed after grounding pass k1 qualified its universal \\\"depends on nothing beyond\\\" clause and its unattributed TET-27 characterization; the paragraph now matches the narrowed claim.\",\"cite\":null,\"timestamp\":1786132480}\n30:{\"event\":\"Revise\",\"id\":\"P1\",\"text\":\"TET-28 design: the modification-target census, and where its refusal can decidably live\",\"why\":\"the markdown prefix was typed into the heading text while --level already supplies it, so render emitted it twice\",\"cite\":null,\"timestamp\":1786135098}\n31:{\"event\":\"Revise\",\"id\":\"P3\",\"text\":\"What \\\"workspace-rooted\\\" can decidably mean\",\"why\":\"the markdown prefix was typed into the heading text while --level already supplies it, so render emitted it twice\",\"cite\":null,\"timestamp\":1786135098}\n32:{\"event\":\"Revise\",\"id\":\"P7\",\"text\":\"The trigger: declaration, not detection\",\"why\":\"the markdown prefix was typed into the heading text while --level already supplies it, so render emitted it twice\",\"cite\":null,\"timestamp\":1786135098}\n33:{\"event\":\"Revise\",\"id\":\"P13\",\"text\":\"The rule, precisely\",\"why\":\"the markdown prefix was typed into the heading text while --level already supplies it, so render emitted it twice\",\"cite\":null,\"timestamp\":1786135098}\n34:{\"event\":\"Revise\",\"id\":\"P15\",\"text\":\"What the census does not see, printed rather than implied\",\"why\":\"the markdown prefix was typed into the heading text while --level already supplies it, so render emitted it twice\",\"cite\":null,\"timestamp\":1786135098}\n35:{\"event\":\"Revise\",\"id\":\"P17\",\"text\":\"Sequencing, and what this memo does not decide\",\"why\":\"the markdown prefix was typed into the heading text while --level already supplies it, so render emitted it twice\",\"cite\":null,\"timestamp\":1786135098}\n3:All code observations in this memo were taken against the tetel repository at commit 67191f5 (\"tet28: pin the census refusal, and make the parity guard real\"); the working tree was clean at the start of authoring, and this memo plus its snapshot are the one addition the workspace makes to it. That commit matters beyond pinning: it means TET-28's modification-target machinery — the record type, the refusing verb, the render section, the snapshot re-verification — is in the tree, and this design reads it as implemented precedent rather than as a sibling design document. TET-29 asks for a second required section: any design that transplants a mechanism from another site must carry a premise inventory — donor site, the donor's comment-stated premises transcribed verbatim, and per premise a claim that it holds at the destination — with `check` refusing the shape (section absent, or a premise row citing no claim) and never judging the content. This memo settles what the trigger is, what the record holds, what \"transcribed verbatim\" can decidably mean, where the refusals fire, and what is printed as uncatchable.\n7:The answer, up front. The ticket survives contact with the codebase, but not in its own vocabulary — four reformulations, each argued below. First, the trigger: \"any design that transplants\" is as undecidable from prose as \"a memo's recommendation\" was for TET-28, so the trigger is an author-declared transplant record, and the refusal attaches to declarations. Second, and largest: \"transcribed verbatim\" cannot be a typed field on trust — a premise is refused unless its text is a verbatim substring of one observation's captured output inside the cited donor fact, which converts transcription from a request into a machine guarantee and makes TET-29 the first load-bearing use of the README's supporting-span-selection direction. Third, the refusal the ticket placed at `check` splits across three surfaces: quotation invariants refuse at the verb, completeness refuses at `render --out`, and `check` re-verifies both against the snapshot for documents that never passed through either. Fourth, a composition the ticket left open: the transplant's destination is a citation of a live modification target, so declaring a transplant forces TET-28's census at the landing site. What none of this touches — deliberately — is content: the tool never decides what counts as a premise, whether a selection is deep enough, or whether a discharge claim is true. Its entire effect is to move the violated property from \"asserted by no claim at all\" into the evidence ledger, where the existing grounding loop can reach it.\n13:The ticket's rule as written — \"any design or fix that transplants a mechanism from another site must carry a premise-inventory section\" — has the same undecidable subject TET-28's ticket had. Nothing in the record model types anything as a transplant; deciding that a fix transplants means either reading the prose for tell-tale language or comparing code across two sites, and both are heuristic reads of content, the class this project has measured dead once (scope.rs's mention-detector flagged a legitimate quotation and missed both real overreaches) and has now shipped a refusal-shaped alternative to once (targets.rs, whose module doc carries the argument as implemented doctrine, not proposal). So the trigger is the same shape: the author declares a transplant as a structured record, the refusal attaches to the declaration, and the memo that transplants without declaring is not detected — it is made visible, by a section that renders its own absence and a non-coverage line that prints the blind spot. This memo does not re-litigate that reasoning; it verifies it transfers, and it does: the one disanalogy — a transplant is an act the design performs, where a modification target is a thing the design names — changes nothing about decidability, because in both cases the undecidable step is deciding from content that the memo is in the rule's scope at all.\n23:Two objections were the reason this could not simply be assumed, and both were settled against the capture code rather than by preference. First, strictness: a donor comment wrapped across lines, with `//` or `*` prefixes and indentation inside it, is in fact a contiguous byte substring of a `look` capture. The plain open stores the file's bytes verbatim — no line numbers, no headers, no reflowing — and the `--lines` mode stores the selected lines re-joined with newlines, normalising CRLF endings and the trailing newline in that mode only; in both modes the relation is defined over the captured string itself, so what a memo can quote is exactly what was captured, never a third spelling. The cost is that the premise appears in the rendered section with its comment syntax intact, which the \"never paraphrase\" constraint treats as a feature: the reader sees the donor's clause as the donor's file spells it. Stripping the noise would require per-language comment knowledge, the exact coupling scope.rs's \"Language-agnostic by construction\" section exists to exclude. The two capture modes that genuinely fight containment — grep outputs, whose lines carry `lineno:` prefixes, and whole-search records, whose output is empty — make `look` of the donor file the natural donor capture, and the refusal's diagnostic should say so; the relation itself stays indifferent to observation kind. Whether the motivating premise itself clears this bar is not left to argument — the next paragraph tests it at the source. Second, soundness: a fact's output is the join of its observations' outputs, boundaries unstored, so whole-output containment would accept text straddling the seam between two captures — bytes never contiguous in anything the author saw. The fix is one serde-defaulted per-entry byte-length field on the extent entry, the same compatibility move that added kind and pattern for TET-28: boundaries become reconstructible, the relation tightens to \"substring of a single observation's output\", and a donor fact minted by an older build is ineligible with the same cheap remedy — look again.\n37:## Where the refusals fire: verb, then render --out, then check\n39:TET-28 moved its whole refusal to the verb and left render refusal-free. TET-29 cannot copy that placement wholesale, and the reason is a real disanalogy, not taste: a census either exists when the target is declared or it does not, but a premise inventory is built across time. The instrument works by transcribe-first — the author selects the donor's premises before knowing whether each holds at the destination; that confrontation is the mechanism. Refusing an undischarged premise at declaration would demand the destination-holds claim before the premise can exist, inverting the order into answer-first-transcribe-after, and pushing an author who cannot yet discharge a premise toward the quiet exit of never declaring it — the exact \"refusing would make the honest case unwritable\" failure the project's own discriminator names. So the refusals split by question. The verb refuses everything that is per-record and decidable at creation: a donor fact that does not exist or predates span boundaries, a premise that is not a genuine quotation, a destination that is not a live target, a discharge naming no live claim. Completeness — every premise on every live transplant discharged — is a property of the finished document, and it is refused at `render --out`: the act that writes the reviewable artifact and its snapshot, under the already-pinned policy that a rendered memo is never hand-edited. A memo with a declared-but-unexamined premise fails to come into existence rather than existing and failing; the remedy is singular; a preview render still assembles with undischarged rows marked loudly, so mid-work visibility survives. This makes TET-29 the first refusal on the render path, which is the design's most debatable departure — the alternative of letting the memo render and having only check fail it matches the ticket's letter, but it manufactures exactly the artifact the ticket exists to prevent and then depends on the author running check. Last, check re-verifies the whole shape against the shipped snapshot, both directions since tampering goes both ways, as a machine failure beside uncensused targets; containment is re-checked byte-for-byte from the facts.jsonl the snapshot already ships, so check still runs nothing and fetches nothing. Without a snapshot, transplant rows are human-owed unverifiable — the standing choice for every snapshot-dependent finding.\n45:The rendered section sits beside the modification-target section and follows its conventions: labels rather than absolute keys, withdrawn records dropped, and — once the workspace has any claim — an empty section that renders as \"none declared\" rather than disappearing, because the hole is the finding and an absent section would make the same omission invisible. Per live transplant, the reader sees the donor fact and its extent labels, the destination target, and each premise's selected bytes verbatim in a fenced block with the discharging claim's id beside it. One shipped rule is deliberately inverted here: compose.rs refuses to inline captured output because output is unbounded, but a premise is an author-bounded selection, and inlining it is the entire point — the reader must see the donor's clause on the page, spelled the way the donor's file spells it, next to the claim that answers it, or the section degenerates into opaque pointers. Fenced blocks rather than table cells, because a premise legitimately spans lines and a markdown cell cannot carry multi-line text without escaping it, which would break the byte-fidelity the whole design is built on.\n59:Every component extends a mechanism already in the tree, and none crosses a doctrinal line: one serde-defaulted per-entry output-length field on the extent record (the compatibility move that added kind and pattern for TET-28, with the same absent-means-ineligible reading — a legacy donor fact is re-looked, never guessed about); one new record kind, counter, and id prefix; a transplants.jsonl written by one CLI/MCP-shared dispatch and shipped by one new line in the snapshot's deliberately-enumerated file list, whose targets.jsonl entry already documents why a record log must ship; one rendered section; one completeness refusal on the render --out path; one snapshot re-verification beside the census one; and the six non-coverage lines. Nothing parses any programming language — the containment check is byte comparison, indifferent to what the bytes mean — nothing is re-run or fetched at check time, and no score or document-level verdict appears anywhere. What this memo deliberately does not decide: the premise-row id spelling and event grammar beyond append-only-with-withdraw; whether span selection should later generalise to ordinary mint-time notes, which stays a Direction item with this feature as its first measurement; and any widening of the trigger toward detecting undeclared transplants, which the measured evidence against prose-reading refusals rules out now for the same reason it did for TET-28.\n78:| C2 | TET-29's trigger must be a declaration, not a detection, by the same argument TET-28 implemented rather than merely designed. \"Any design or fix that transplants a mechanism from another site\" denotes no enumerable set of records: nothing in the record model types a claim or prose block as a transplant, and deciding from English (or from comparing code across two sites) that a fix \"transplants\" is a heuristic read — the class scope.rs measured dead for symbol mentions (\"Naming a symbol the code you read calls is normal; concluding about what that symbol *does* is the failure — and those two are not distinguishable by string matching\") and the class targets.rs's shipped doc comment rules out as a refusal trigger. So a transplant is a record the author declares, the refusal attaches to the declaration, and under-declaration is handled the way TET-28 handles it: an always-rendered section that shows its hole, plus a printed non-coverage line, never a detector. | OWED |\n80:| C4 | The strictness objections to byte-level containment dissolve on the actual capture path, and every weaker relation reopens the invention channel. A donor comment wrapped across lines with interior prefix noise (`//`, `*`, indentation) IS a contiguous byte substring of a look_path capture: the plain open stores the file's bytes verbatim, and the --lines mode stores the selected lines re-joined with \"\\n\" (normalising CRLF endings and the trailing newline in that mode only) — in both modes the premise relation is defined over the captured String itself, so what a memo can quote is exactly what was captured, never a third spelling. The cost is only that the selected premise carries its comment syntax into the rendered section, which the \"never paraphrase\" constraint endorses rather than tolerates. Stripping that noise would require knowing each language's comment syntax, which is precisely the hard-coded per-language coupling scope.rs's own \"Language-agnostic by construction\" doctrine section excludes (\"There is no list of languages and no list of extensions\"; the rejected alternative, a hard-coded extension-to-language map, is \"exactly the coupling this avoids\"). Two capture modes genuinely fight containment — grep-derived match outputs interleave \"lineno:\" prefixes between lines, and whole-search/no-match records carry empty output — so the design's guidance is that a donor fact is minted from `look` of the donor file; that is guidance about which observation to select from, not a new rule, since the relation is indifferent to which kind produced the bytes. Weaker relations fail: per-line containment lets an author stitch non-adjacent lines into a sentence the donor never wrote, and whitespace- or case-normalised matching means the memo shows text the capture does not contain — both are paraphrase with extra steps. | OWED |\n85:| C9 | The refusals land on three surfaces, each the earliest that can decide its own question. (1) At the verb: quotation invariants — donor fact exists and is span-eligible, premise text is a verbatim substring of a single observation's output, destination is a live target, a discharge names an existing live claim. These are per-record and decidable at creation, exactly like target's census refusal. (2) At `render --out`: completeness — a live transplant with an undischarged premise (none cited, or its discharge claim withdrawn) refuses the write. This is deliberately later than the verb (the intermediate state is legal while authoring) and deliberately earlier than check (the ticket's placement): --out is the act that writes the reviewable artifact and its snapshot under the pinned policy that a rendered memo is never hand-edited, so a memo with a declared-but-unexamined premise should fail to come into existence rather than exist and fail; the remedy is singular — discharge the premise, or withdraw it or its transplant with --why. A preview render (no --out) still assembles, marking undischarged rows loudly, so mid-work visibility is preserved; this render/--out asymmetry is new, and it is the design's most debatable departure. (3) At check: re-verification of everything above against the shipped snapshot — both directions, since tampering can go either way (a transplant row the snapshot lacks; a snapshot premise whose text is not contained in its donor fact's output; a premise whose discharge claim is withdrawn or missing) — as a machine failure in the same partition as uncensused targets, with rows in a snapshotless document reported human-owed as unverifiable, the standing choice for snapshot-dependent findings. Containment is re-checked byte-for-byte from facts.jsonl, which already ships full outputs; check runs nothing and fetches nothing to do it. | OWED |\n87:| C11 | The rendered section follows compose.rs's shipped conventions with one deliberate difference. A \"## Transplants\" section renders beside the modification-target section: per live transplant, the donor fact id with its extent labels (labels, never absolute keys — the committed-document rule), the destination target id and symbol, and one entry per live premise carrying the selected text verbatim in a fenced block with the discharging claim id beside it; withdrawn transplants and premises are dropped, and once any claim exists the section renders even when empty (\"none declared\"), because the hole is the finding — the same visibility answer to under-declaration the target section shipped. The deliberate difference: compose.rs refuses to inline captured output into the document because output is unbounded, but a premise is an author-bounded selection, not a whole capture, so inlining it verbatim is both safe and the entire point — the reader must see the donor's clause on the page, spelled the way the donor's file spells it, or the section is a list of opaque pointers. Fenced blocks rather than table cells because a premise legitimately spans lines and markdown table cells cannot carry multi-line verbatim text without escaping, which would violate byte-fidelity. | OWED |\n90:| C14 | The implementation surface is small and every piece extends a mechanism already in the tree: one serde-defaulted per-entry output-length field on ExtentEntry (the same compatibility precedent that added kind and pattern, with the same absent-means-ineligible reading); one new record kind with its counter and id prefix; one transplants.jsonl appended by one CLI/MCP-shared dispatch, shipped by adding one line to snapshot.rs's deliberately-enumerated SNAPSHOT_FILES (whose targets.jsonl entry already documents why a record log must ship: without it the refusal \"would hold only inside the workspace that authored the memo\"); one rendered section beside render_targets; one completeness refusal in the render --out path; one check re-verifying from files the snapshot already carries (transplant machine findings beside uncensused_targets, unverifiable rows human-owed without a snapshot); and the new NON_COVERAGE lines. Nothing parses any programming language, nothing is re-run at check time, and no aggregate, score, or document-level verdict is introduced anywhere. | OWED |\n104:| F7 | The assembly-and-verification surfaces TET-29 extends, first-hand. compose.rs: render appends render_targets between the prose and the evidence ledger; the section renders \"None declared...\" once any claim exists (\"the hole is the finding\" — \"an absent section would make the same omission invisible, which is the failure mode, not the fix\"); withdrawn targets are dropped, live ones render as a two-column table \\|`symbol`\\|F#\\|; only labels are rendered, never absolute keys (\"a committed document should not carry /Users/<someone>/...\"), and captured output is deliberately NOT rendered into the document (\"unbounded ... it is in the snapshot for anyone who needs it, and a document that inlined it would bury the prose\"). checks.rs: Findings has uncensused_targets as a MACHINE failure (\"re-verified against the shipped snapshot ... what this catches is the document that never passed through that verb: hand-authored, edited after rendering, or carrying a target row its own snapshot does not have. Both directions are checked\"), and unverifiable_targets as human-owed when no snapshot shipped; machine_check_failed() ORs grammar/subset/abutting/unsettled/cascade/ledger/verdict-disagreement/out-of-proof/uncensused-targets/provenance; out_of_proof (every record grades a wording the claim no longer carries) is a machine failure whose remedy is reproving. report.rs: exit codes 0/1/2; NON_COVERAGE is a const list printed item by item (\"tetel does not catch: ...\"), and TET-28 added five census entries to it with a comment explaining why (\"a refusal that fired is easily read as coverage it never claimed\"); first entry is \"dependents that never declared themselves\". | /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/compose.rs lines 1-130; /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/checks.rs lines 120-215; /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/report.rs lines 13-40 | C9, C11, C12, C13, C14 |\n105:| F8 | snapshot.rs first-hand: SNAPSHOT_FILES is a deliberately enumerated list — \"a future workspace file is a deliberate decision to ship or withhold, not an accident of cp -r\" — currently [facts.jsonl, claims.jsonl, prose.jsonl, targets.jsonl, counters.json, pending.json, refusals.log, identity.json]. targets.jsonl's own comment states the pattern TET-29 must repeat: \"Shipped, not withheld: without it check can see the rendered target rows but nothing to verify them against, and the census refusal would hold only inside the workspace that authored the memo — exactly the reviewer-of-a-committed-document case the snapshot exists for.\" So shipping a transplants.jsonl is one line in this array, and check then re-verifies premise containment from facts.jsonl (which carries full Create events including output) plus the transplant log. The module also pins the policy \"a rendered memo is never hand-edited\" and mints workspace identity in write() itself so no caller can forget it. | /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/snapshot.rs lines 55-145 | C9, C14 |\n3:{\"_type\":\"https://in-toto.io/Statement/v1\",\"subject\":[{\"name\":\"C2\",\"digest\":{\"sha256\":\"2c0e5be90dc995b5d4f30e0eb5b7ab7454ba1d5a462c490b199a7ad06de02977\"}}],\"predicateType\":\"https://github.com/lonic-software/tetel/captured-fact/v1\",\"predicate\":{\"verdict\":\"qualifies\",\"pass\":\"068c02e043beda16\",\"reported_kind\":\"run\",\"source\":\"proc:workspace-068c02e043beda16\",\"extent\":[\"proc: grep -n -e distinguishable -e language-agnostic -e unwritable -e honest case /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/scope.rs (exit 0)\",\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/scope.rs lines 30-90\",\"proc: grep -rn -e concluding about it is the failure -e language-agnostic by construction /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/README.md (exit 0)\",\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/scope.rs lines 240-330\"],\"world\":[{\"root\":\"/Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel\",\"state\":\"git:5076b5fe177f2ab87259d8d1e36881c055e0be35001992b01789e8ac000461a8\"}],\"note\":\"The argument holds in substance and every structural clause checks out: nothing in src types anything as a transplant, scope.rs did measure the symbol-mention detector dead, and targets.rs's shipped doc comment rules out deciding-from-English as a refusal trigger, handling under-declaration by an always-rendered section plus non-coverage line. But the sentence C2 quotes and attributes to scope.rs is not scope.rs's text: scope.rs (lines 68-69) says \\\"concluding about what that symbol *does* is the failure — and those two are not distinguishable by string matching\\\", while the quoted \\\"concluding about it is the failure\\\" wording is targets.rs line 21-22's paraphrase, and \\\"the two are not distinguishable\\\" matches neither byte-for-byte. In a design whose subject is verbatim quotation versus paraphrase, a spliced quote presented as scope.rs's words is the clause I have to name.\",\"pin\":\"sha256:4b46528ff1e93c6d1849b8dc81efb94ef943961c2296ff179623e03b14f19260\",\"timestamp\":1786174404}}\n13:{\"_type\":\"https://in-toto.io/Statement/v1\",\"subject\":[{\"name\":\"C8\",\"digest\":{\"sha256\":\"219bb7ee1154ad24ff7ec8c213b1c6d0249aca5f09dcda3e7cba39c3597ffd64\"}}],\"predicateType\":\"https://github.com/lonic-software/tetel/captured-fact/v1\",\"predicate\":{\"verdict\":\"supports\",\"pass\":\"068c02e043beda16\",\"reported_kind\":\"run\",\"source\":\"proc:workspace-068c02e043beda16\",\"extent\":[\"proc: ls /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src (exit 0)\",\"proc: wc -l /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/targets.rs /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/scope.rs /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/compose.rs /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/snapshot.rs /Volumes/SSD/Documents/lonic/tetel/src/fac