65:Everything the implementer touches is declared in the modification-target section above, and there are six symbols: a field on `Findings`; a new `prose_after_proof`; the call to it inside `check_file`'s existing snapshot block, whose other occupants are the note-versus-extent check, the tree-state findings, the census and premise re-verifications and the mint windows; a `block_offsets` in `compose` that `render` consumes; the print loop and one added clause in the human-owed scope sentence; and the new `NON_COVERAGE` entries. That block is where this finding's neighbours live; it is not everything in `check` that touches a snapshot, since the drift check and the authoring-identity read reach into one from outside it. One instruction in the list is worth stating precisely, because getting it wrong fails silently. The check has two inputs: the snapshot's `prose.jsonl`, read directly because the loader discards timestamps, and the claims and evidence `check_file` already holds. Those claims must be the ones `check_file` imports from the rendered document, never the snapshot's `claims.jsonl` — every digest on record was computed over a ledger-derived proposition, and the two are not the same string, since rendering replaces newlines with spaces and importing trims every cell. Implemented against the workspace log, the check would find no matching digest for such a claim, conclude it is not in proof, and drop every block citing it, with nothing red to say so. `machine_check_failed` is untouched, which is what makes the line human-owed by construction rather than by good intentions. Nothing new ships in the snapshot; no schema changes; nothing is executed and no file the document names is opened, so `check`'s standing guarantees hold unchanged. Without a snapshot the list is simply not computed, in silence, exactly as the note-versus-extent check and the mint windows are not. Five things go into the printed non-coverage list. False prose that ships alongside a claim revision, which the ticket concedes and this design does not fix. A paragraph that cites nothing. A citation added to a claim not yet in proof, which cannot be listed, because by the time any pass proves that claim its first proof postdates the edit. Whether the two clocks agree. And whether the prose is wrong, which nothing here judges and nothing here should.
69:One measurement made in passing deserves recording, because the next ticket in this family will meet it. Declaring these six targets required six whole-worktree searches, and a census captures its entire output into a fact that ships in the snapshot. This repository's previous two snapshots hold whole file dumps on single JSON lines — tet29's longest is 57249 bytes, tet28's 34477 — so a search for a symbol this crate discusses re-captures those dumps. What such a search costs turns out to be a property of the instrument and the moment rather than of the symbol, in two separate ways. Scope: `render` matches 486 lines and 373271 bytes of this tree in the state the census was taken in, or 447 and 311672 with `.git/` and `target/` excluded as well; `NON_COVERAGE` 35 and 95637, `Findings` 23 and 57418, `check_file` 5 and 285, and the two symbols that do not exist yet nothing at all. Those numbers hold still because the trees they range over are frozen. Time: the same counts taken with this memo's own artifacts included do not hold still at all. `render` measured 529 lines and 1136689 bytes at one point in this memo's review and 550 and 1202134 later, with no source change in between — what grew was this memo's evidence log, now 59 records and 164392 bytes, and its snapshot, whose longest fact line is the 574898-byte capture of the `render` census itself. Grading a claim about census cost enlarges the thing the claim is about, which is the compounding thesis demonstrating itself. It is also the one part of this section no independent pass can confirm, along with the count of six searches: both range over this memo's own logs and snapshot, which a pass grading this memo must not open, so they are recorded here expecting to stay ungraded. What any pass can check, and what survives every way of scoping the count, is the ordering and the direction: censusing a symbol this crate talks about costs orders of magnitude more than censusing one it does not, and every memo added under `docs/design/` enlarges the next memo's census of anything it discusses.
