nes  1202134 bytes\nNON_COVERAGE  whole tree as it stands now:   63 lines   767119 bytes\nFindings      whole tree as it stands now:   49 lines   723216 bytes\nthis memo evidence log now: 59 records, 164392 bytes\n","pin":"sha256:2bd4b85216b57a039501ac712cf115d1c2184d9bfe27371583de202ef3195abb","timestamp":1786194293}
17:{"event":"Create","id":"P17","heading":false,"level":null,"text":"Everything the implementer touches is declared in the modification-target section above, and there are six symbols: a field on `Findings`; a new `prose_after_proof` that computes the list from the snapshot's prose and claim logs plus the evidence records `check_file` has already loaded; the call to it inside `check_file`'s existing snapshot block, beside the other findings that only exist when a snapshot shipped; a `block_offsets` in `compose` that `render` consumes; the print loop and one added clause in the human-owed scope sentence; and the new `NON_COVERAGE` entries. `machine_check_failed` is untouched, which is what makes the line human-owed by construction rather than by good intentions. Nothing new ships in the snapshot, because both logs this reads are already in it; no schema changes; nothing is executed and no file the document names is opened, so `check`'s standing guarantees hold unchanged. Without a snapshot the list is simply not computed, in silence, exactly as the note-versus-extent check and the mint windows are not, and the bullet that already reports a missing snapshot explains why. Four things go into the printed non-coverage list. False prose that ships alongside a claim revision, which the ticket concedes and this design does not fix. A paragraph that cites nothing, which has no edge to compare against at all. Whether the two clocks agree. And whether the prose is wrong, which nothing here judges and nothing here should.","cite":["C11"],"before":null,"timestamp":1786190077}
20:{"event":"Create","id":"P20","heading":false,"level":null,"text":"One measurement made in passing deserves recording, because the next ticket in this family will meet it. Declaring these six targets required six whole-worktree searches, and a census captures its entire output into a fact that ships in the snapshot. This repository's previous two snapshots hold whole file dumps on single JSON lines, the longest 57110 bytes, so a search for a symbol this crate discusses re-captures those dumps: `render` came back as 486 matching lines and 405019 characters, `NON_COVERAGE` as 35 lines and 98828, `Findings` as 23 lines and 59.2 KB, against `check_file`'s 5 lines and 285 bytes. Two things follow. The larger captures exceeded what the authoring surface returns, so the symbol populations were established by the searches while their individual sites went unread here — the census fixes rooting and extent, never that anyone inspected each site, which is a thing the census's own non-coverage list already says in another form. And the cost compounds: every memo added under `docs/design/` enlarges the next memo's census of any symbol it discusses.","cite":["C13"],"before":null,"timestamp":1786190148}
32:{"event":"Revise","id":"P20","text":"One measurement made in passing deserves recording, because the next ticket in this family will meet it. Declaring these six targets required six whole-worktree searches, and a census captures its entire output into a fact that ships in the snapshot. This repository's previous two snapshots hold whole file dumps on single JSON lines, the longest 57110 bytes, so a search for a symbol this crate discusses re-captures those dumps. What that costs turns out not to be a property of the symbol at all but of the instrument and the moment, and the numbers say so plainly: `render` matches 486 lines and 373271 bytes of this tree as it stood when the census ran, 529 lines and 1136689 bytes once this memo's own artifacts exist, and 447 and 311672 if `.git/` and `target/` are excluded too. `NON_COVERAGE` goes from 35 lines and 95637 bytes to 54 and 737911 across the same before-and-after; `Findings` from 23 and 57418 to 41 and 698345. Two of the six symbols do not exist yet and are censused by the searches that found nothing, and `check_file` costs 5 lines and 285 bytes. What survives every way of scoping the count is the ordering and the direction: censusing a symbol this crate talks about costs orders of magnitude more than censusing one it does not, and every memo added under `docs/design/` enlarges the next memo's census of anything it discusses.","why":"C13 was rewritten after k1 could not reproduce three of its four byte figures; the measurements now show the figures are instrument- and moment-dependent, which is a sharper version of the same point. The paragraph quoted the unreproducible numbers as if they were properties of the symbols.","cite":["C13"],"timestamp":1786191853}
38:{"event":"Revise","id":"P17","text":"Everything the implementer touches is declared in the modification-target section above, and there are six symbols: a field on `Findings`; a new `prose_after_proof`; the call to it inside `check_file`'s existing snapshot block, beside the other findings that only exist when a snapshot shipped; a `block_offsets` in `compose` that `render` consumes; the print loop and one added clause in the human-owed scope sentence; and the new `NON_COVERAGE` entries. One instruction in that list is worth stating precisely, because getting it wrong fails silently. The check has two inputs: the snapshot's `prose.jsonl`, read directly because the loader discards timestamps, and the claims and evidence `check_file` already holds. Those claims must be the ones `check_file` imports from the rendered document, never the snapshot's `claims.jsonl` — every digest on record was computed over a ledger-derived proposition, and the two are not the same string, since rendering replaces newlines with spaces and importing trims every cell. Implemented against the workspace log, the check would find no matching digest for such a claim, conclude it is not in proof, and drop every block citing it, with nothing red to say so. `machine_check_failed` is untouched, which is what makes the line human-owed by construction rather than by good intentions. Nothing new ships in the snapshot; no schema changes; nothing is executed and no file the document names is opened, so `check`'s standing guarantees hold unchanged. Without a snapshot the list is simply not computed, in silence, exactly as the note-versus-extent check and the mint windows are not. Five things go into the printed non-coverage list. False prose that ships alongside a claim revision, which the ticket concedes and this design does not fix. A paragraph that cites nothing. A citation added to a claim not yet in proof, which cannot be listed, because by the time any pass proves that claim its first proof postdates the edit. Whether the two clocks agree. And whether the prose is wrong, which nothing here judges and nothing here should.","why":"C11 was corrected after attack pass a1: the build order named claims.jsonl as an input, but check_file imports its claims from the rendered document and that is what every proposition digest was computed over, with ledger_cell and split_row_cells not round-tripping newlines or edge whitespace. The non-coverage list also gained an entry.","cite":["C11"],"timestamp":1786192887}
